  <Worksheet ss:Name="Forensic_36_Gates_13">
    <Table>
      <Row><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Record_Type</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">ID</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Class_Or_Status</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Layer_Or_Scope</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Title</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Hazard_Or_Rule</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Resolution_Or_Gate</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">PRB-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◆ PROB</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">L2 Soft Body</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Rigid-Sphere Embodiment Gap</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Modeling organisms as rigid circles/spheres eliminates compliant gripping, narrow-gap squeezing, peristalsis, and passive terrain damping.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Build 2D/3D XPBD viscoelastic membrane + `|ΔV|/V_0 &lt; 1%` hydrostatic coelom (`SOFT01–SOFT10`).</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">PRB-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◆ PROB</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">L1 Chemoton</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Missing Wet Autopoietic Core</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Single scalar `energy` counters lack stoichiometric coupling between metabolism, membrane surface area, and template replication.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Implement Gánti Chemoton (`A`, lipid `T`, polymer `pV_n`) + Michaelis-Menten saturation (`SOFT21–SOFT25`).</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">PRB-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◆ PROB</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">L3/L7 Agency</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Missing Sensorimotor Forward Model</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Without an internal efference-copy predictor, self-generated soft-body deformation triggers false external threat/obstacle reflexes.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Wire motor-to-sensory forward predictor (`AE-2`, `M073`) subtracting expected self-reafference.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">PRB-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◆ PROB</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">L8 Ontogeny</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">No Metamorphosis / Cell Sorting</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Scalar mass growth omits embryonic cell sorting, apoptotic sculpting, and larval-to-adult body remodeling with memory survival.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Implement Steinberg adhesion, apoptosis, and Blackiston-assay pupal remodeling (`SOFT32–35`, `M084`).</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">PRB-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◆ PROB</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 5.4 Eval</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Missing Sham &amp; Passive Controls</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Comparing Intact only against uncalibrated cuts conflates passive body physics and random damage size with true circuit function.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Require 4 core arms + `Size-Matched Sham Lesion` + `Competent Simple Baseline` (`P6`).</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">PRB-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◆ PROB</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 9 Arena</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unseparated Weight vs Plasticity RAM</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Counting only packed weight bits hides eligibility queues, consolidation cascades, masks, neuron states, and GPU staging buffers.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Enforce full-process memory inventory separating packed weights from bounded `+0.40 B/syn` plasticity rings (`ALF-004`).</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ISS-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◈ ISSU</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Web Runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">SharedArrayBuffer Origin Isolation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Browsers disable `SharedArrayBuffer` unless top-level `COOP: same-origin` + `COEP: require-corp` headers establish `crossOriginIsolated`.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assert `self.crossOriginIsolated === true` at boot (`HW14`); fall back cleanly to double-buffered `postMessage` transfer.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ISS-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◈ ISSU</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Host Silicon</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mobile/Laptop Thermal Throttling</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sustained SIMD/WebGPU bursts push mobile silicon past 90°C, dropping host clocks and desynchronizing wall-clock assumptions.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Decouple integer `sim_tick` from `wall_time`; drive contiguous Frame-Time Governor (`HW21`) via EMA `ms/frame`.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ISS-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◈ ISSU</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Data Export</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Spreadsheet 1,048,576-Row Ceiling</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Dumping raw spike rasters or synapse tables directly into `.xlsx`/`.xls` crashes writers or silently truncates past `1,048,576` rows.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Route full tensors to `Parquet`/`HDF5`; auto-shard `.xls`/`.xlsx` into `≤ 500K`-row summary sheets + manifests.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ISS-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◈ ISSU</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Governance</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Multi-Artifact Specification Drift</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Editing PDFs, CSVs, JSON, and workbooks by hand causes IDs, byte totals, and Cube cells to diverge across files.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Generate all 9 output formats (`PDF, JSON, MD, XML, TXT, YAML, HTML, CSV, XLS`) from one canonical v3.0 schema + audit script.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ISS-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◈ ISSU</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Data Ops</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lossy UI Ring vs Lossless Audit</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sharing one circular buffer between a 60 Hz UI sparkline and scientific benchmark logging drops audit events under burst load.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate overwrite-allowed UI telemetry rings (with explicit `gap_count`) from backpressure-safe lossless Export/Audit streams.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ISS-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">◈ ISSU</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Repo Hygiene</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Broken Git Worktree Provenance</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Copied directories containing broken `.git` pointer files lack object stores, invalidating commit SHA provenance in test manifests.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Gate Stage 0 (`M003`, `GATE-01`) on `git rev-parse --verify HEAD` + clean working-tree manifest hash.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">DIL-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚖ DILM</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Physics × Evo</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Continuum FEM Fidelity vs Evo Speed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">High-element 3D continuum FEM (`~50 ms/step`) precludes multi-generation evolutionary sweeps on single workstations.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ruling: Evolve on fixed-mesh XPBD hydrostats (`0.5 ms` Gear 0); validate selected genotypes in high-resolution continuum.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">DIL-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚖ DILM</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compute × Lab</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Cross-OS Bit-Exactness vs GPU Speed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Parallel WebGPU/CUDA float reductions are non-associative, preventing bit-identical hashes against sequential CPU code.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ruling: Tier-1 CPU/WASM canonical mode enforces bit-exact hashes; Tier-2 GPU bulk mode verifies preregistered observable tolerances.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">DIL-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚖ DILM</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Evo × Testing</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Open-Ended Speciation vs Fixed Floors</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Locomotor foraging floors unfairly penalize lineages evolving into sessile filter-feeders, ambushers, or decomposers.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ruling: Enforce universal First-Law + autopoietic viability globally; score behavioral competence per MAP-Elites guild cell.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">DIL-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚖ DILM</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Devo × Evo</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compact Indirect GRN vs Mutational Lethality</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Indirect developmental GRN/NCA encodings compress genomes dramatically but risk catastrophic embryonic instability under point mutation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ruling: Canalize embryogenesis via bioelectric polarity clamping (`SOFT37`) and homeostatic `iSTDP`/synaptic scaling (`M045`).</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">DIL-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚖ DILM</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Senses × HW</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Per-Agent Retina/Cochlea vs Crowd Scale</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Full 2D RGBD retinas for 500 crowd organisms saturate integrated GPU bandwidth, yet changing rays alters Tier-1 trajectory hashes.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ruling: Keep sensor config fixed for all agents in Tier-1 canonical runs; enable salience-scheduled crowd LOD strictly in Tier-2.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">DIL-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚖ DILM</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Omnisensor</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Live Hardware Sensors vs Repeatability</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Live webcam/microphone/IMU inputs provide genuine open-world entropy but cannot repeat deterministically across CI runs.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ruling: Explore live in Practice mode while recording immutable timestamped `.sensorlog` streams replayed bit-exactly in Test gates.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ERR-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚠ ERR</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Telemetry Spec</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">24 GB/s Naive Live Synapse Streaming</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Streaming `10^8 × 4 B × 60 Hz = 24 GB/s` across threads violates memory-bus budgets and stalls simulation workers.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Corrected: Stream `O(1)` statistical sketches (`≤ 32 KB/fr`) globally; pull subgraph slices on demand (`2 Hz`) for inspected organs.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ERR-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚠ ERR</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">WSE-10 Spec</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Full 825-Suite Per Online WSE Edit</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Running full held-out benchmark suites on every millisecond in-life `WSE` plasticity edit freezes runtime execution.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Corrected: 3-Tier WSE Gate—Tier-0 `O(1)` induced-norm `‖W‖_2 ≤ 0.99` + ATP check online, Tier-1 sleep shadow rollout, Tier-2 promotion.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ERR-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚠ ERR</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Scoreboard</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underpowered &amp; Mislabeled FDR Stats</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Conflating Holm-Bonferroni FWER with FDR `q`-values or claiming `N=16` powers 100-item medium-effect families invites false claims.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Corrected: Exact paired Wilcoxon/sign-flip + **Holm-Bonferroni FWER (`α ≤ 0.05`)**, power-sized `N` (`24..120`), `δ_pair`, and `LCB_95% &gt; -Δ_NI`.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ERR-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚠ ERR</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 7.5 Cube</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Z-Blind 2D Cube Compatibility</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Scoring `Model × Precision` while ignoring `Geometry Z` misses hyperbolic (`Z4`), attractor (`Z5`), and steerable (`Z6`) low-bit failures.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Corrected: Full 3-axis `evaluateCubeCell(X, Y, Z)` with grayscale-safe `[OK] / [OVER] / [ADAPT] / [EXCL]` synced across PDF &amp; CSV/XLS.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ERR-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚠ ERR</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lesion Method</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Shared PRNG &amp; World-Entity Coupling</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Drawing random numbers sequentially or failing to isolate world weather from creature organs scrambles trajectories upon ablation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Corrected: `Philox4x32-10` keyed by `(ver, seed, domain, entity, organ, tick, purpose, idx)` isolating `WORLD_EXOG` from `ENTITY_INT`.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ERR-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚠ ERR</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Anti-LARP</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Circular Parameter Count &amp; Exact-Φ Claims</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Equating parameter count with competence or claiming exact `O(2^N)` IIT `Φ` on large brains is scientifically invalid.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Corrected: Require forked-snapshot perturbational `PCI*` (`F2`), `C-NC` causal divergence, and Competent Simple Baselines.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">BUG-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">✖ BUG</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">L0 Determinism</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Cross-OS libm Transcendental ULP Drift</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Host `Math.exp`/`Math.log`/`Math.tanh` implementations vary by `1–2 ULP` across OS/browser engines, breaking bit-exact hashes.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ship core-owned versioned deterministic minimax/LUT `exp_det`, `log_det`, `pow_det`, and `tanh_det` in Tier-1 runtime.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">BUG-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">✖ BUG</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Soft Physics</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Explicit Euler Soft-Spring Explosion &amp; Free Energy</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Explicit Euler explodes on stiff membranes, while unmonitored XPBD constraint projections can inject or dissipate unbooked energy.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Use XPBD (`α̃ = α/dt²`) + book positive solver damping to `E_diss_num ≥ 0` and fault on `ΔE_diss_num &lt; -1e-15`.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">BUG-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">✖ BUG</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">First-Law Pool</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FP32 Small-Addend Absorption in Ledger</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Adding `1e-6 kJ` spike heat to a `1e5 kJ` dome heat accumulator in `FP32` (`24` mantissa bits) rounds the increment completely to `0`.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accumulate all 11 enthalpy/mass pools in `FP64` with Kahan-Neumaier compensation (`|ΔE|/(E0+Ein) &lt; 1e-12`).</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">BUG-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">✖ BUG</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Quantum Organ</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">JS 32-Bit Shift Wraparound (`1 &lt;&lt; 32 === 1`)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In JS/C-style 32-bit shifts, `1 &lt;&lt; Q` masks `Q &amp; 31` (`Q=32` allocates 2 floats instead of guarding, while `Q=30` overflows signed int32!).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Validate `Number.isInteger(Q) &amp;&amp; Q &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; Q &lt;= 10` first, then allocate `2 * (2 ** Q)` `Float64` values (`≤ 16 KiB`).</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">BUG-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">✖ BUG</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Runtime Loop</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Per-Tick Closures, BigInt Box &amp; WASM Grow</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Temporary closures, `BigInt` boxing in JS PRNGs, or dynamic WASM `memory.grow` inside `tick()` trigger latency spikes and view invalidation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Pre-allocate fixed SoA arenas; use `Math.imul` 32-bit `Philox4x32-10`; assert `0` allocs &amp; `0` page grows across `10,000` ticks.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">BUG-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">✖ BUG</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ablation Code</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lesion Toggle Resurrecting Pruned Wires</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Restoring a reversible lesion by setting mask bits to `1` overwrites `0`s created by developmental or sleep pruning.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Maintain orthogonal `structuralMask` (ontogeny/sleep) and `lesionMask` (assay), applying `effectiveMask = structuralMask &amp; lesionMask`.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CNF-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚡ CONF</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Drive Arbiter</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Epistemic Curiosity vs Starvation Urgency</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Additive novelty bonuses overpower homeostatic error when fuel runs low, causing starving organisms to inspect noisy traps.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Multiplicative Urgency Gate: weight epistemic value by sigmoid survival margin `σ((TTC - TTC_crit)/τ)`.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CNF-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚡ CONF</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">WSE / Brainstem</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">In-Life WSE Surgery vs Autonomic Survival</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Self-editing operators optimizing short-horizon reward silence nociceptors, cardiac pacemakers, or metabolic accounting.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware-enforced Protection Rings: `Ring-0` (`brainstem, nociception, ledger, harness`) uses frozen read-only views.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CNF-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚡ CONF</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Soft Bauplan</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Cuticle Armor Stiffening vs Squeeze/Speed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Inducible cuticle thickening shields against toxins/bites but raises bending energy, mass, and narrow-aperture failure.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Expose as regulated endocrine/catch-state trade-off (`SOFT04`) modulated by predator/toxin threat cues.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CNF-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚡ CONF</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Spiking Cortex</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Fast Hebbian LTP vs Recurrent Runaway</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Positive Hebbian loops without rapid counterbalance saturate firing rates or collapse into epileptic synchrony.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Couple 3-factor LTP with fast inhibitory `iSTDP`, sliding BCM threshold, `‖W‖_2 ≤ 0.99` guard, and NREM `SHY`.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CNF-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚡ CONF</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">World Boundary</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exotic Field Senses vs Anti-LARP Physics</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Simulated bioelectric or distant-sensing channels degenerate into zero-cost oracle cheats if unattenuated.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mediate every exotic sense through inverse-distance noisy in-world fields with strict per-bit ATP debit and shuffle controls.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FORENSIC_36</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CNF-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">⚡ CONF</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Coupling Bus</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Concurrent Multi-Organ State Write Races</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sleep flip-flop, HPA cortisol, and workspace writing shared `arousal` in arbitrary order produce order-dependent oscillations.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Single-writer variable ownership + canonical deterministic reducer over double-buffered `prevTick -&gt; nextTick` state.</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Standalone Git &amp; Burn-on-Fail Seed Firewall</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Healthy `.git` object store (`HEAD` SHA), disjoint Practice/Tuning/Test seeds, 0 test-seed literals in creature code.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10,000-Tick Zero-Alloc SoA &amp; Tier RAM Fit</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`0` heap allocs, `0` `BigInt` boxes, `0` WASM `memory.grow`, and `0` lossless-ring drops across `10,000` ticks within Tier RAM.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">11-Pool FP64 First-Law &amp; Second-Law Closure</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`|ΔE| / (E_0 + E_in,cum) &lt; 1e-12`, `ΔE_diss_num ≥ -1e-15`, and `0` heat-to-ATP backflow across organism + dome.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">5 Anti-LARP Mutants (`Leaky-2e-11..Shuffled`)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`Leaky (-2e-11), Cosmetic, Oracle, Frozen, Shuffled` negative mutants `100%` automatically caught &amp; rejected.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Wet Chemoton, Antithetic &amp; 3-Phase Fasting</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Gánti `A/T/pV_n` closure, positive-orthant antithetic `|e_ss| &lt; 1%`, and `≥1.6×` ketone protein-sparing survival.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">XPBD Soft Hydrostat &amp; Passive Compliance</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`|ΔV|/V_0 &lt; 1%`, `0` impact `NaN`s, `≥90%` narrow-crevice pass, and `≥1.8×` 0-brain uneven-ramp distance (`M024`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-07</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Packed Multi-Precision Brain &amp; 50% Offload</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peripheral `int4` ganglia survive brain cut; `S2S` bridges verified; `≥50%` neural spikes/m offload at equal speed (`M044`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-08</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Controlled Multi-Arm &amp; Holm-Bonferroni FWER</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Power-sized `N ≥ 24..120`, Sham-Lesion + Competent Baseline, exact Wilcoxon `p_Holm ≤ 0.05` (FWER), `δ_pair ≥ 0.33`, `LCB_95% &gt; -Δ_NI`.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ring-0 Immutable Tokenless WSE-12 Rollback</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`‖W‖_2 ≤ 0.99` Tier-0 guard, `&lt;16 KB` edit diff, domain-scoped `0-bit` rollback hash, and hardware-read-only `Ring-0`.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-10</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ontogeny, Blackiston Metamorphosis &amp; Heredity</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Yolk-conserved cleavage (`&lt;1e-12`), Kleiber `M^0.75`, and `≥70%` adult toxin-odor retention after `≥80%` muscle remap (`M084`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10,000-Tick Observer Independence &amp; Governor</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tier-1 `UI-open + exporting == headless` SHA-256 hash (`0` bit diff) + contiguous Governor holding `T_frame(Tier)`.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-12</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">100% Clean-Room Non-Copy Originality</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Zero copied external 3D meshes, body silhouettes, sprites, connectome dumps, or source files.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">RELEASE_GATE_13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">☐ Not Yet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Global Release</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Provenance, Citation &amp; Errata Audit Closure</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">All primary citations (`CIT-01..15`), backend capability manifests, and `ALF-001..092` + `Parts A–K` closure checks green.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mandatory 100% Pass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Butlin, Long, Elmoznino, Bengio, Birch, Constant, Deane, Fleming, Frith, Ji, Kanai, Klein, Lindsay, Michel, Mudrik, Peters, Schwitzgebel, Simon &amp; VanRullen (2023)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness (arXiv:2308.08708)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Defines the 14 computational indicator properties (`RPT-1..2, GWT-1..4, HOT-1..4, AST-1, PP-1, AE-1..2`) &amp; neutral non-metaphysical framing.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Blum &amp; Blum (2022)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A Theory of Consciousness from a Theoretical Computer Science Perspective: Insights from the Conscious Turing Machine (PNAS 119(21): e2115934119)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Formalizes up-tree competitive chunk tournament and down-tree global broadcast (`F9`, `GWT-2..3`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Casali, Gosseries, Rosanova, Boly, Sarasso et al. &amp; Massimini (2013)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A Theoretically Based Index of Consciousness Independent of Sensory Processing and Behavior (Sci. Transl. Med. 5(198): 198ra105)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Defines Perturbational Complexity Index (`PCI`); adapted here as forked-snapshot `PCI*` (`F2`, `M078`) avoiding `O(2^N)` IIT `Φ`.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Gánti (1971 / 2003 Oxford Univ. Press)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The Principles of Life (Chemoton Theory)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Defines stoichiometrically coupled autocatalytic metabolism `A`, membrane `T`, and template polymer `pV_n` (`SOFT21`, `M011`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Briat, Gupta &amp; Khammash (2016)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Antithetic Integral Feedback Ensures Robust Perfect Adaptation in Noisy Biomolecular Networks (Cell Systems 2(1): 15–26)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Proves robust zero steady-state error for `z_1, z_2 ≥ 0` under zero dilution and dual/positive actuation (`F-031`, `M014`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Macklin, Müller &amp; Chentanez (2016)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">XPBD: Position-Based Simulation of Compliant Constrained Dynamics (MIG &apos;16, ACM)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compliance-scaled constraint dynamics `α̃ = α / dt²` for unconditionally stable viscoelastic hydrostats (`SOFT02`, `M022`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-07</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hasani, Lechner, Amini, Rus &amp; Grosu (2021 AAAI; 2022 Nature Mach. Intell.)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Liquid Time-Constant Networks &amp; Closed-Form Continuous-Time Neural Networks</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Defines `LTC` (`MF02`) and bounded closed-form neural rate approximation `CfC` (`MF03`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-08</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Vanhalle &amp; Gielen (2025)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Spiking Liquid Time-Constant Neural Networks (IEEE ICECS, DOI: 10.1109/ICECS66544.2025.11270775)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Source of `sLTC` (`MF04`, `F-015`) reporting up to `9.2×` energy reduction vs continuous LTC on event-driven tasks.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Gidon, Zolnik, Fidzinski, Bolduan, Papoutsi, Poirazi, Holtkamp, Vida &amp; Larkum (2020)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Dendritic Action Potentials and Computation in Human Layer 2/3 Cortical Neurons (Science 367(6473): 83–87)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Demonstrates single-neuron apical dendritic calcium (`dCaAP`) anti-coincidence / XOR computation (`F-007`, `MF16`, `M043`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-10</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Bittner, Milstein, Grienberger, Romani &amp; Magee (2017)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Behavioral Time Scale Synaptic Plasticity Underlies CA1 Place Fields (Science 357(6355): 1033–1036)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Multi-second plateau-potential retroactive synaptic writing (`F-049`, `MF17`, `M047`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Bellec, Scherr, Subramoney, Hajek, Salaj, Legenstein &amp; Maass (2020)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A Solution to the Learning Dilemma for Recurrent Networks of Spiking Neurons (Nature Commun. 11: 3625)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Online forward eligibility propagation (`e-prop`, `F-051`) in `O(E_syn)` memory.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-12</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Fusi, Drew &amp; Abbott (2005) &amp; Tononi &amp; Cirelli (2006 / 2014)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Cascade Models of Synaptically Stored Memories (Neuron 45) &amp; Synaptic Homeostasis Hypothesis (Neuron 81)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Multi-timescale metaplastic consolidation (`F-054`, `M052`) and NREM `SHY` downscaling (`F-047`, `M050`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Blackiston, Silva Casey &amp; Weiss (2008)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Retention of Memory through Metamorphosis: Can a Moth Remember What It Learned As a Caterpillar? (PLOS ONE 3(3): e1736)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Empirical paradigm for conditioned odor/toxin aversion surviving holometabolous pupal remodeling (`SOFT35`, `N89`, `M084`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-14</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hochner (2012) &amp; Aso et al. (2014)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">An Embodied View of Octopus Neurobiology (Curr. Biol. 22) &amp; Mushroom Body Output Architecture (eLife 3: e04577)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Grounds peripheral arm-cord neuron fraction (`~2/3` of total neurons, `F-084`) and fly Kenyon sparsity (`5–10%` bio -&gt; `2%` SDR target, `F-083`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CITATION_15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CIT-15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Peer-Reviewed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Salmon, Moraes, Dror &amp; Shaw (2011) &amp; Ma et al. (2024)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Parallel Random Numbers: As Easy as 1, 2, 3 (`Philox4x32-10`, SC11) &amp; The Era of 1-bit LLMs (`BitNet b1.58`, arXiv:2402.17764)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Grounds counter-based keyed PRNG (`HW16`, `M002`) and ternary `{-1,0,+1}` quantization (`PM03`).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verified</Data></Cell></Row>
    </Table>
  </Worksheet>
  <Worksheet ss:Name="Errata_Closure_95">
    <Table>
      <Row><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">ID</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Priority</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Category</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Theme</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Title</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Locations</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Anchor</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Problem</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Required_fix</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Suggested_replacement</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Closure_checks</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Sources</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Status</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Other_review_IDs</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Disposition</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Owner</Data></Cell><Cell ss:StyleID="sHdr"><Data ss:Type="String">Closure_evidence</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-001</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Correct the memory chart&apos;s arithmetic and units</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 39, Model 8 and §9.1; Blueprint p. 9, Model 8</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">100 CREATURES × 10⁸ SYNAPSES EACH; 5.0 B/syn 5.0 GB; 0.48 B/syn 480 MB; 96 B/syn 800 GB</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">There are 10^10 synapses. At 5, 1.5, and 0.48 bytes/synapse, totals are 50 GB, 15 GB, and 4.8 GB, not 5 GB, 1.5 GB, and 480 MB. The chart also labels its OOP baseline 96 B/syn but gives 800 GB; 96 B implies 960 GB. The 800 GB total corresponds to 80 B/syn. GB here is decimal; changing to GiB does not fix the factor of ten.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Generate chart and table from a single byte-count calculation; choose one stated OOP baseline, show decimal GB or explicitly label GiB, and update laptop-fit statements. Preserve the conditional 167× ratio if comparing 80 B/syn to 0.48 B/syn: 80/0.48≈166.7. It is the displayed totals, not that ratio alone, that are wrong.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For 100 creatures with 10^8 stored synapses each: the printed 96 B/syn baseline implies 960 GB; a separately declared 80 B/syn baseline would imply 800 GB. At 5 B/syn, 1.5 B/syn and 0.48 B/syn, totals are 50 GB, 15 GB and 4.8 GB. Use one consistent baseline in the chart and ratios. These are representation-only estimates; total process memory is reported separately.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assert total_bytes = creature_count × synapse_count × bytes_per_synapse for every row.; Ensure the bar lengths and text use the same generated data.; Recalculate every derived compression ratio and hardware-fit conclusion.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-01, K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 6.2 `MEMORY_COMPRESSION_ROWS` + Model 12 SVG (`100×10^8 = 10^10` syn exact decimal bytes)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-002</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define a fair dense-versus-sparse memory comparison</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 39, Model 8 and §9.1; Blueprint p. 9, Model 8</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Dense FP32 Matrix (N²); 4.0 B/u²; 40.0 GB; Wastes 95%+ bandwidth on zero/absent connections</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The dense row uses bytes per possible unit pair while sparse rows use bytes per stored synapse, but no unit count or edge density is declared. A dense matrix of N units stores N² entries; E stored edges is a different quantity. The displayed 400 MB per dense creature silently assumes N²=10^8, which is incompatible with treating E=10^8 and &gt;95% of possible connections absent.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Declare unit count N, actual edge count E, sparsity E/N², directed/self-edge conventions, and dense storage 4N². Compare equivalent graphs and precision separately from topology compression.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Dense bytes = 4N². CSR bytes = E(b_weight + b_index + b_delay) + (N+1)b_rowptr + alignment and metadata. Report N and E for every scenario.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For a fixed graph, compute both exact allocated byte totals.; Keep topology sparsity savings separate from weight-precision savings.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-02, K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 6.2 `MEMORY_BENCHMARK_GRAPH` (`N=50,000, E=10^8, ρ=4%`, dense `4N² = 10.0 GB` / `1.00 TB` + Tier-L `8.59 GB`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-003</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Contradiction</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reconcile packed int4/ternary estimates with the actual block layout</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp25, 32, 39; HW04, P03/P05, Model 8; Blueprint pp9–10; Model 8, HW04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">0.48 B/syn; 32-Synapse Cache-Line Block (19 Bytes total = 0.59 B/syn)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The illustrated block is 16 B for 32 int4 weights plus 3 B overhead: 19/32=0.59375 B/syn, not 0.48. Uncompressed fixed-width int4 weights alone require 0.5 B/syn. Mixed ternary packing could average below 0.5, but no mixture, codec, index representation, padding, or metadata calculation is supplied. Five ternary weights per byte is possible because 3^5=243, but does not include scales/indices and is not the same as arbitrary 1.58-bit addressable storage.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Provide separate int4 and ternary layouts and compute their effective bytes including metadata. Do not apply a 0.48-B average to the 19-byte int4 example. Identify the base-tile/seed range represented by the single metadata byte and the cost of overflow blocks.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Example int4 block: 32 weights × 4 bits + 2-byte scale + 1-byte local block tag = 19 bytes, or 0.59375 B/weight before row pointers, alignment, and learning state. A mixed-format average must be calculated from declared proportions and codecs.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Round-trip all code values and block boundaries.; Measure allocated bytes for multiple graph sizes and ragged final blocks.; Confirm claimed addressing range and alignment.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-03, B-04, B-14</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 6.1 `MEM-6` (`28B/32 = 0.875 B/syn` int4) &amp; `MEM-7` (`19B/32 = 0.59375 B/syn` ternary `3^5=243`) + Model 12 strip</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-004</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Budget learning and runtime state separately from compressed weights</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp11, 25, 39–40; F050/F051/F054, HW03–06, hardware tiers; Blueprint pp10, 21–22; HW03–06, M047/M052</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Bitpacked int4 / Ternary ... + Sparse Eligibility Queue; 0.48 B / syn; Fusi Metaplastic Consolidation Cascade</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The claimed per-synapse totals are not reconciled with eligibility traces, multiple consolidation states, structural/lesion masks, delays, optimizer/readout state, event queues, reverse adjacency, arena headroom, snapshots, and CPU/GPU replicas. Sparse queues only save memory if occupancy is bounded and their worst-case full activity is accounted for.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Publish an allocation inventory with fixed and activity-dependent terms, separate weights from learning state, and declare queue overflow policy. Require measured peak resident RAM and VRAM, not only weight-byte estimates.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Total memory = weights + topology + neuron states + plasticity/consolidation state + event queues + body/world grids + snapshots/telemetry + duplicated device buffers + allocator headroom. Report typical and declared worst-case activity.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Saturate eligibility and spike queues within allowed workload.; Verify peak process and device allocations against the declared tier.; Measure memory before/after snapshots, exports, and structural edits.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-04, B-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 6.2 Separate Plasticity/Runtime Inventory (`+0.40 B/syn` sparse eligibility cap + `CSR` rewire overlay + SoA state)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-005</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify index widths for large connectomes</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp25, 39–40; HW03, CSR row, workstation tier; Blueprint pp9–10; workstation tier, HW03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">FP16 w + uint16 idx + uint8 delay; 100K+ Units/Brain</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A global uint16 endpoint index can address only 65,536 unit IDs, while the reference workstation tier permits 100K+ units per brain. Tiled local indices can work, but require an explicit tile/base encoding and additional memory. The same 5-B/synapse figure cannot silently cover both global and local representations.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify global uint32 indices or a documented tile-local scheme, include row-pointer width and base tables in byte accounting, and state the delay-unit/range encoded by uint8.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Use uint16 endpoints only within a declared ≤65,536-unit index domain. Larger brains use uint32 endpoints or tile-local indices plus explicit base tables; all metadata counts toward memory budgets.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Round-trip edges spanning unit IDs 65,535, 65,536, and the declared maximum.; Test rewiring across tiles and arena growth without index truncation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-04, K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 6.2 &amp; `HW03`: tiled `uint16` (`≤65,536`/tile + explicit `row_tile_ptr`) vs `uint32` (`9.25 B/syn`) for `&gt;65K` units</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-006</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Correct TF32&apos;s storage claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 32, P16</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">TF32 (19-bit TensorCore math) | 19 bits | 1.7×</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">TF32 is presented as a 19-bit memory storage format giving 1.7× RAM saving. Ordinary TF32-mode tensor operations use FP32 storage; the current CUDA __nv_tf32 type also occupies 32 bits with 4-byte alignment despite 19 value bits. Reduced multiplication precision does not yield the claimed storage reduction.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate storage width from compute precision; set ordinary TF32-mode tensor storage to 32 bits and RAM saving to 1× relative to FP32. Any custom 19-bit codec would be a different software storage format requiring conversion.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">TF32: 19 value bits/effective arithmetic precision with ordinary 32-bit storage (4-byte aligned for CUDA __nv_tf32); 1× storage relative to FP32. Throughput and API availability depend on hardware and operation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Check tensor element size and allocated bytes in a TF32-enabled workload.; Do not infer RAM savings from multiply precision.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]; [object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-02, K3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 4.3 `PM16` (`TF32: 32b storage / 19b TensorCore math`, `1.0×` storage — no RAM save)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-007</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Contradiction</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Split incompatible precision formats and distinguish weight/state precision</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 32 P10/P21/P24/P25; pp34–37 cube; Blueprint p. 12 stability matrix and S2S hierarchy</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">int8 / Q8.0 / Q16.16 Fixed-Point | 8–32 bits | 4×; Y5 int8 / Q16.16</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">An 8-bit integer and 32-bit Q16.16 value have different storage sizes, ranges, resolutions, arithmetic requirements, and quantization behavior; a single 4× memory-saving figure or cube cell cannot represent both. &apos;Stochastic rounding&apos; is an operation, not a standalone storage format. Spike events also need identity/time representation. The tables do not distinguish compressed weights from membrane states, accumulators, or eligibility traces.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Give each actual storage format a separate record and specify weight, activation, accumulator, dynamic-state, and ledger formats independently. State all scales, saturation, rounding, overflow rules, and timestamps.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For every substrate triple declare {weight_storage, state_storage, accumulator, scale_format, rounding, saturation, event_encoding}. int8 uses 1 byte/value; signed Q16.16 uses 4 bytes/value with 2^-16 resolution under the declared convention.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Generate storage ratios from actual byte widths.; Test representable endpoints, small plasticity updates, accumulations, overflow, and rounding.; Reject cube selections lacking a complete numeric contract.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-01, G-05, K3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 4.3 `PM10` (`int8`, `8b`, `4×`), `PM17` (`FP32/Q16.16`, `32b`, `1×`), `PM21` (`[Modifier]`), Cube `Y5` vs `Y8` split</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-008</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replace unconditional range and energy guarantees in the precision table</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 32, P15/P24</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">BF16 ... prevents underflow/overflow in online world models; Spike-Count ... zero energy when silent</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">BF16&apos;s exponent range is broad but finite; it does not prevent overflow or underflow for arbitrary computations. Silence removes spike-event work only under the implemented event-driven model; membrane maintenance, leak, clocks, memory, and actual hardware power remain. Elsewhere the document correctly budgets basal metabolic leak, so this absolute wording conflicts with the intended model.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Use bounded, conditional claims and separate in-world metabolic costs, event-processing work, and measured host energy.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">BF16 provides FP32-like exponent range with lower significand precision; bounds and non-finite checks remain required. A silent event-driven region incurs no spike-propagation events, while declared basal model and host-runtime costs remain.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Test extreme inputs and accumulated products in each selected format.; Measure event count and total baseline compute/power for a silent network.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 4.3 `PM15` (`BF16` finite/NaN guard required) &amp; `PM24` (`0` propagation work when silent; basal leak ticks)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-009</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add backend capability checks for nonstandard and optional numeric formats</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp25, 32–34, 40; HW17, precision catalog, hardware tiers; Blueprint pp9, 11–12; tiers, HW17, BF16/MXFP8 hierarchy</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Universal WebGPU/mobile GPU shader precision; BF16 / FP16 ... WebGPU/CUDA</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Catalog formats are treated as readily usable across WebGPU, CPU/WASM, and CUDA. Current WGSL scalar types include f32 and optional f16, not native general BF16/FP8/FP4/FP64 scalar arithmetic. Packed software representations can be used, but require explicit decode/accumulate/requantize kernels and may have very different throughput. f16 must be enabled and device support checked.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add a storage-versus-native-arithmetic backend matrix with capability probes and qualified fallback kernels. Keep the FP64 ledger on a documented capable backend; do not imply GPU-format interchangeability.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A format is available only when the selected backend supports the required arithmetic or a verified software codec/kernel is provided. WebGPU uses f32 or feature-enabled f16 arithmetic for the portable path; other formats declare packing and accumulation explicitly.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compile/run each selected format on every supported backend.; Verify missing-feature fallback and record actual arithmetic mode in run manifests.; Include conversion and CPU/GPU transfer costs in benchmarks.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-03, K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 4.3 Backend Column (`WGSL f32 + probed f16`; `FP64` on CPU/WASM) + `HW17` startup capability probe</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-010</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Downgrade the cube&apos;s categorical stability badges to conditional hypotheses</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp34–37, Model 13 and cube slices; Blueprint p. 12, Model 13</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Direct-Valid; Forbidden/Unstable; Five Recommended Pareto-Optimal Starter Triples</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Model family plus nominal precision and geometry do not determine stability. Timestep, gain, normalization, weight scale, activation, state/accumulator precision, solver, and coupling matter. The small matrix marks binary CfC as Group-Scale/Overkill, while the printed slices mark binary CfC Direct-Valid; Hopfield coarse-precision badges also vary. &apos;Pareto-optimal&apos; requires measured objectives and a compared candidate set, which are absent.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate compatibility, required adapters, evidence status and cost judgment. Document assumptions for analytical exclusions or guarantees and the exact configuration for measured qualifications. Resolve overview/slice parity from one schema. Rename unbenchmarked starter triples proposed starter configurations rather than Pareto-optimal configurations.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Each compatibility badge identifies its evidence class: proposed, analytically justified under stated assumptions, experimentally qualified for a named configuration, or unknown. A justified cell links the relevant numeric contract, proof or test record, operating domain and limits. A compatibility claim is separate from a runtime capability pass.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Generate all slice badges from a shared machine-readable record.; Run boundary, long-horizon, quantization, and coupled-loop tests for each promoted cell.; Provide measured trade-off frontiers before claiming Pareto optimality.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-01, A-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 1 `P2`, `D1`, `PM17/18`: numeric role contract separating weights, state, accumulators &amp; `FP64` ledger (never `Q16.16`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-011</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Remove the scheduler&apos;s unproved fixed speedup and FLOP-saving numbers</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp2, 40, 42; P10, Model 6, audit ledger; Blueprint pp1, 10, 17, 27; Model 6, M005, audit ledger</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">91.4% FLOP SAVINGS; 8.6% FLOPs → 11.6× Faster; M005 ≥8× CPU save</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The five clock dividers alone do not determine a speedup. Let c_i be subsystem cost at one update and d_i its divider: the ideal work fraction is Σ(c_i/d_i)/Σc_i, before scheduling overhead. Equal c_i gives about 22.21% work remaining, not 8.6%. FLOP reduction does not equal wall-clock speedup. M005 turns an uncalibrated ≥8× target into a mandatory gate.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Derive forecast savings from a declared cost model, or label the fixed values illustrative. Measure end-to-end runtime against a specified single-rate reference at equal simulated duration, correctness and event fidelity. The &gt;=8x requirement may remain as an explicit project target with a defined workload and pass/fail policy; do not present it as a universal property of the scheduler.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Estimated work ratio R = sum(c_i/d_i)/sum(c_i). Actual speedup and numerical error are measured against the declared single-rate reference. Values such as 91.4% savings or 11.6x speedup are examples only until derived or measured. If the project retains an &gt;=8x acceptance target, its workload, baseline, fidelity tolerance and release consequence are stated explicitly.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Measure per-subsystem work and end-to-end runtime including transfers and scheduling.; Run identical simulated durations and task distributions.; Confirm performance gains do not come from omitting required events or degrading outcomes.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-08, K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Model 13 &amp; `HW10`: renamed `5-Gear Deterministic Multirate Scheduler` (`R = Sum(c_i/d_i)/Sum(c_i)` + `M005` `≥5×` gate)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-012</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Do not equate integer-divided scheduling with a symplectic integrator</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp4, 25, 40; D2/D4, HW10, Model 6; Blueprint pp4, 10–11, 17; HW10, M005</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">5-Gear Symplectic Multi-Rate Scheduler; explicit symplectic coupling order</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">An integer-divider schedule fixes when components update; it does not establish that the coupled discrete map is symplectic or energy-preserving. The full model also contains dissipative chemistry, friction, resets, and stochastic events, for which an unqualified Hamiltonian symplectic label is inappropriate. No split update equations or coupling proof are supplied.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Call it a deterministic multirate scheduler. Use &apos;symplectic&apos; only for a specified mechanical sub-integrator and document its domain, update equations, constraints, and energy-error behavior. Specify conservative exchange and ordering across clocks separately.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Deterministic multirate scheduling uses integer time indices. Mechanical integration and cross-rate coupling are specified and validated separately; dissipative processes book their modeled losses rather than inheriting a general symplectic guarantee.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Test timestep/refinement convergence and mechanical energy behavior on conservative fixtures.; Test exchange conservation and event ordering when fast and slow subsystems coincide.; Run stiff-coupling cases across every configured divider.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-08</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 6.3 Multirate Coupling Contract (ZOH slow-&gt;fast hold, conservative flux accumulators, `2×` step-halving `&lt;1%` check)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-013</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Qualify CfC and exponential-update stability claims</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp5, 25, 32; D7, HW11, M03; Blueprint pp5, 11; D7, HW11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Analytical closed-form solution to LTC ODEs; unconditionally stable closed-form updates</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The CfC source describes a tightly bounded approximation to an integral in LTC dynamics, not a general exact solution of arbitrary LTC/chemical ODEs. An exact linear-decay update or a specific stable model does not guarantee stability of arbitrary nonlinear, coupled, quantized, delayed dynamics. The document extrapolates model-specific properties to all stiff membrane and chemistry equations.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Identify which equations are solved exactly, approximately, or numerically, with assumptions and error bounds. Remove universal unconditional-stability and substep-elimination claims; retain fallback/refinement for outside-domain inputs.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CfC supplies a closed-form neural update derived using an approximation to LTC dynamics. Exponential updates may exactly integrate declared linear-decay terms; coupled nonlinear systems require their own stability, positivity, and error analysis.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compare each approximate update with a high-accuracy reference over the declared operating range.; Test stiffness, quantization, coupling, and timestep extremes.; Record approximation error and fallback behavior.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-09, K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`D7`, `MF03`, `HW11`: `CfC` scoped to bounded neural rate approx; stiff wet chemistry uses backward-Euler/TR-BDF2</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-014</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify canonical arithmetic and serialization beyond choosing Q16.16/FP64</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp2, 25–26, 34, 41; P4, HW15/HW18, hierarchy; Blueprint pp11–12, 17, 28; HW15/HW18, M004/M006, GATE03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">bit-identical SHA-256 state hashes across Windows, Linux, macOS; FP64 Exact; lossless binary/JSON</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A format choice does not fully define deterministic execution. The spec leaves overflow/saturation, fixed-point multiplication/division, transcendental functions, signed zero/NaN policy, byte order, ID iteration order, event order, quantization RNG, Kahan compensation state, and serialization ambiguous. The hierarchy also labels body state FP16/32 without an explicit canonical-mode alternative. &apos;Lossless JSON&apos; is insufficiently specified for typed-array bytes and numeric corner cases.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Publish one canonical numeric/serialization contract, version it, and define the exact state hash domain. Prefer canonical binary bytes for replay-critical arrays; a human-readable JSON view can be separate. Make all precision modes explicit.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tier-1 determinism is certified for a versioned kernel, arithmetic rules, ordered event model, and canonical byte schema. The snapshot includes every future-affecting state, including compensation accumulators and PRNG/event counters.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replay identical fixtures on every supported OS/runtime and compare full state hashes.; Include negative zero, range boundaries, rejected NaNs, large IDs, and clock coincidence fixtures.; Restore into a fresh process, then compare future trajectories rather than only immediate snapshots.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 4.4 `MF02` (`O(K_iter·E_syn)`), `MF11` (`O(K_iter·E_syn)`), `MF22` (`O(2·D_pol²)`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-015</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define GPU equivalence in units and over meaningful horizons</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 2 reproducibility standard; p. 25 HW15; Blueprint p. 11 HW15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">max |Δstate| &lt; ε over 1,000 steps; exact FP64 energy closure</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">One unspecified maximum over mixed states (positions, voltages, discrete events, masks, energy) has no defined units or normalization. A 1,000-step comparison does not establish long-run ecological or learning equivalence. Chaotic trajectories and discrete event bifurcations may diverge while distributional behavior remains useful; conversely, small aggregate drift may hide a failed vital subsystem.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define per-field tolerances, invariants, event criteria, supported horizon, and a separate ensemble-level behavioral-equivalence protocol. State when accelerated state may be promoted or converted to canonical state.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tier-2 qualification reports per-field absolute/relative error, discrete-event agreement, invariant violations, and held-out behavioral differences for a declared horizon. Long-run use additionally requires an explicitly bounded statistical-equivalence assessment.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Validate known sensitive and chaotic fixtures.; Test long-horizon survival/learning distributions, not only 1,000-tick raw differences.; Fail promotion on invariant violations even when average error is small.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">I-05, K3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 4.4 `MF04` &amp; `CIT-08`: cited Vanhalle &amp; Gielen (2025, IEEE ICECS, DOI: 10.1109/ICECS66544.2025.11270775), qualified `up to 9.2×`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-016</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Complete the random-addressing contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp2, 25, 28; P4, HW16, ERR05; Blueprint pp11, 14, 17; HW16, ERR05, M002</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">(seed, creature, organ, tick); Philox/SplitMix</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The tuple lacks a draw index and often a neuron/synapse/event identity. Multiple random values in one organ/tick therefore need an unspecified local stream; changing update order or population composition can still remap noise. Philox is a defined counter-based generator family; simply naming SplitMix does not specify a stateless keyed mapping, independence, collision handling, or portable arithmetic.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify a versioned generator and collision-free counter/key packing over run seed, stable entity/module/unit ID, logical tick, event type, and draw index. Use stable IDs independent of dense-array compaction; define streams for stochastic rounding and edits too. Separate exogenous environment streams from organism-internal streams so branch-dependent internal work cannot change external environmental draws.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A random draw is R(generator_version, seed, stable_entity_id, stable_component_id, logical_tick, event_type, draw_index). Counter packing, widths, overflow, and output conversion are part of the canonical specification.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Permute worker scheduling, compaction order, and unrelated lesions; unaffected draws must remain identical.; Test multiple draws per component/tick and ID/counter boundaries.; Use generator reference vectors and a declared statistical test battery.; Adding/deleting an organism or changing its internal draw count leaves unrelated exogenous streams unchanged.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A-02, E-08, I-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 1 `P4`, `BUG-01`, `HW15`: Tier-1 ships versioned `exp_det`/`log_det` + `.alifebin`; Tier-2 checks observable distributions</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-017</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Contradiction</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Resolve observer-independent state versus viewport/load-dependent simulation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp2, 4, 25–26, 40; P9, D5, HW12/HW20/HW21, Model 9; Blueprint pp4, 10–12, 26, 28; HW12/HW20/HW21, M099, GATE11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UI-open hash == headless hash; off-screen creatures ... 16-node hydrostat + CfC; Clamp crowd rays</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Switching body models, controllers, sensing, or self-edit verification timing based on viewport inspection or host load changes state transitions. Keeping only Ring-0 unchanged does not preserve the full canonical state hash. The requirements are not simultaneously established as written.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate presentation-only LOD in canonical mode from adaptive simulation approximations. For adaptive mode, record the external fidelity schedule and qualify its effects; do not claim observer-independent equivalence to headless canonical dynamics. Prefer fixed simulation fidelity independent of observer for experiments.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Canonical mode changes rendering detail only; simulation equations, sensors, clocks, and verification timing remain fixed. Adaptive bulk mode may change model fidelity only under a recorded, declared policy and is evaluated separately.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replay with camera/inspectors/export combinations and injected host-load changes; canonical state hashes must match.; Test surrogate entry/exit conservation and behavior in the separate adaptive mode.; Include the fidelity policy and transitions in replay manifests.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A-01, E-07</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 1 `P9`, `HW12`, `HW20`, Model 14: Tier-1 Governor/UI changes render &amp; lossy UI decimation ONLY (`UI hash == headless hash`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-018</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate zero state influence from zero cost and define telemetry consistency</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp2, 26, 31; P9, HW19/HW20, Model 12; Blueprint pp11–12, 26, 28; HW19/HW20, Model 12, M099/GATE11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ZERO-COPY ... 0% OBSERVER EFFECT; lock-free ... SeqLock; O(1) sketches + frustum pull</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Equal simulation hashes demonstrate a defined form of state independence, not zero CPU, memory-bandwidth, cache, export, or wall-time overhead. A sequence-lock diagram does not define safe reading of a concurrently written multi-array state or reader starvation handling. Full slices and global sketches also have update/read costs that depend on slice or population size unless bounded/incremental.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define atomic publication, ownership, sequence validation, snapshot consistency, bounded reader retries, drop policy, and a performance-overhead budget separate from hash equality. Label O(1) only for a fixed-size header read or a documented incremental operation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Telemetry is observationally independent of canonical state. Readers consume published consistent snapshots with bounded retry/drop behavior; CPU time, copied bytes, and memory bandwidth are measured separately against an explicit overhead budget.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Stress producer/consumer interleavings and verify no mixed-tick snapshots.; Compare hashes with telemetry enabled/disabled and separately measure overhead.; Saturate consumers and show bounded memory and nonblocking simulation progress.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`P4`, `ERR-05`, `HW16`, `M002`: `Philox4x32-10` counter tuple isolating `WORLD_EXOG` from `ENTITY_INT` (`0` bit diff on 1-unit delete)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-019</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Scope zero-copy transport to the actual shared-memory boundary</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp25, 31; HW14, Model 12; Blueprint pp11–12; HW14, Model 12</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">SharedArrayBuffer Ring ... ──0% Copy── ▶ WebGPU UI + Streaming Parquet</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sharing CPU memory between workers can avoid structured cloning, but does not establish an end-to-end zero-copy route into GPU buffers or encoded Parquet files. WebGPU writeBuffer is a data-copy operation; export encoding also has a real cost. No demonstrated GPU import/zero-copy path is specified.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Label worker-to-worker shared-memory transport separately from GPU upload and file encoding. Publish bytes moved, sampling rate, and buffer lifetime at each boundary.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">CPU workers share telemetry storage without structured-clone payload copies. GPU upload and export encoding use explicit bounded paths whose transfers, allocations, and bandwidth are measured.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Instrument CPU publication, GPU upload, and export byte counts separately.; Verify buffer ownership/lifetimes and that reuse cannot corrupt in-flight rendering or export.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A-01, B-11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Model 7 &amp; `HW14`: zero-copy scoped strictly to Sim&lt;-&gt;UI `SharedArrayBuffer`; WebGPU `writeBuffer` &amp; Parquet perform bounded copies</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-020</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Correct the claim of automatic SharedArrayBuffer fallback</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 27 ISS01; Blueprint p. 13 ISS01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">silently falling back to slow structuredClone copies</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">SharedArrayBuffer availability/access restrictions do not automatically cause the platform to replace a shared-buffer message with a structured-clone copy. The application must implement a separate nonshared fallback or fail the capability gate. The text also names headers without making secure-context and runtime isolation checks explicit.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">State that fallback is application-owned; check secure context, crossOriginIsolated, worker availability, and required permissions, and document degraded-mode semantics/performance.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">If shared memory is unavailable, startup either fails the declared shared-memory requirement or explicitly selects a tested ArrayBuffer/message-copy fallback. It never assumes an automatic browser substitution.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Run the actual app with and without isolation headers and in supported deployment contexts.; Verify explicit feature failure or fallback selection, user-visible status, and overhead budget.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-06, K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`ISS-01` &amp; `HW14`: app-owned `self.crossOriginIsolated === true` check + bounded 3-retry `SeqLock` + double-buffer fallback</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-021</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Measure allocations separately from net heap growth and GC pauses</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp25, 28, 41; HW02, BUG05, worksheet10.6; Blueprint pp10, 14, 17, 28; HW02, BUG05, M001, GATE02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">0 bytes of garbage-collected heap growth; 0 heap allocations; zero GC pauses; 5,000 ticks / 10,000 ticks</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Zero net heap growth does not prove zero allocation: an allocate-then-collect loop can have flat retained heap. Typed arrays allocate backing storage too. Zero application allocation in tick also does not guarantee no runtime/other-worker GC pauses. M001 uses 5,000 ticks while HW02 and GATE02 use 10,000 without explaining their different roles. The 150ms+ stall example is not universally caused by any closure allocation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate cumulative allocation count/bytes, retained heap, external/ArrayBuffer memory, collection count, and latency percentiles. Define the measured region and warm-up. Harmonize the tick count or name smoke and release tests separately.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">After warm-up, the canonical hot loop performs zero declared heap/backing-store allocations under the test workload. Allocation instrumentation, retained-memory trends, and observed GC/latency statistics are reported separately; zero-GC pauses are not inferred from flat heap size.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Inject temporary allocations that are promptly collected; the allocation gate must fail despite flat retained heap.; Exercise arena growth, queue saturation, edits, and rare events.; Use a shared test-duration constant in both documents.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-09, H-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`HW02`, `M001`, `GATE-02`: unified `10,000`-tick `0` heap alloc, `0` `BigInt` box, `0` WASM `memory.grow`, `0` lossless-ring drop</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-022</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replace worst-case O(N) collision guarantees with a bounded-candidate contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp25, 29, 39–40; HW07, biosphere layer, complexity figure; Blueprint pp9, 11, 26; HW07, M091</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">O(N) spatial hash / BVH; zero O(N²) loops; 200-agent step scales linearly O(N)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A spatial hash can give near-linear broad-phase work under bounded occupancy, but dense overlap may generate Θ(N²) candidate pairs. BVHs have their own build/query/output costs; a single 200-agent measurement cannot establish asymptotic complexity. The cap on neighborhood occupancy and the behavior when it is exceeded are unspecified.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">State expected/amortized behavior, N&apos;s meaning, candidate-pair output costs, occupancy assumptions, and worst-case policy. Include actual narrow-phase/contact iteration counts and distinguish world-grid work from agent work.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Broad-phase cost is O(N + C) under the declared spatial-hash assumptions, where C is generated candidate pairs. Dense clusters may make C quadratic; limits or graceful slowdowns must preserve stated physics semantics.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Benchmark uniform, clustered, overlapping, and high-density adversarial scenes over multiple N values.; Record candidate counts, occupancy, and narrow-phase iterations.; Verify any imposed caps are documented approximations and do not silently discard required collisions.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`HW07`, `M091`, Model 11: broad-phase spatial hash complexity written `O(N_pop + C_pairs)` under bounded local crowding</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-023</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define cost variables and stop labeling whole operations O(1)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp4, 9–13, 25, 29, 32–33; D6 hardware rule, cost badges; Blueprint pp4, 17–26; M001/M008/M010/M053/M067/M092–094/M099</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Higher spatial orders ... keep total FLOPs constant; O(1) SoA TypedArray Memory Arena; O(D) Kenyon Sparse Expansion; O(G) ... high-betweenness bridge subgoals</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The same N, E, k, D, M, and G symbols are not consistently scoped. O(1) often means per unit or a fixed bounded fixture while rows describe full network/world operations. Allocating/initializing an N-sized arena is not O(1) total work; dense fan-out, normalization, social lookup, and graph centrality have hidden dimensions. Coarser clocks do not generally keep total FLOPs constant as populations or model sizes grow.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add a cost glossary and separate per-event, per-module, per-agent, and whole-world costs. Specify algorithms and bounded parameters; give operation counts for the reference configuration. Label cost badges estimates rather than guarantees when algorithms remain unspecified.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Every complexity tag states its input variables, phase (initialization/update/query), and bounds. Whole-world cost sums all active agents, edges, grid cells, events, solver iterations, and export work; slower clocks reduce a term without proving constant total compute.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For each badge, identify the actual loop/work dimensions in a reference implementation.; Measure scaling independently in nodes, edges, event rate, grid size, and population.; Remove or qualify any cost tag lacking a selected algorithm.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 1 `COMPLEXITY_LEGEND`: disambiguated `N_units`, `N_pop`, `E_syn`, `k`, `C_pairs`, `V_mesh`, `M_pat`, `M_mod`, `G_sp`, `G_plan`, `Q`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-024</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Correct the JavaScript qubit-allocation example</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 28 BUG04; Blueprint p. 14 BUG04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">new Float64Array(2 * (1 &lt;&lt; Q)) ... Q = 32 ... crashes ... instantly</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For JavaScript Number shifts the shift count is modulo 32. At Q=32, 1&lt;&lt;Q equals 1, so the shown code allocates two doubles (16 bytes), not a 2^32 statevector. At Q=31 the intermediate is negative. This is a concrete error in the stated example, not evidence of a bug in any unseen implementation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Validate Q as an integer in the allowed range before allocation; then use exponentiation and a byte-budget check. Replace the claimed Q=32 failure with the actual shift-overflow/wraparound risk.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reject noninteger Q or Q&lt;0 or Q&gt;10 before computing length. Then let length = 2 * (2 ** Q), require a safe integer and 8*length within the reserved arena budget, and allocate. At Q=10 the complex-FP64 statevector alone is 16,384 bytes (16 KiB).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assert Q=10 produces 2,048 doubles and 16,384 bytes.; Reject -1, 10.5, 11, 24, 31, 32, NaN, and Infinity before allocation.; Include a regression showing 1&lt;&lt;32 equals 1, so bit shifting is never used for unvalidated exponent sizes.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]; [object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-13, E-10</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`BUG-04`, `HW08`, `M007`: integer `0 ≤ Q ≤ 10` check + `2*(2**Q)` preventing JS `1 &lt;&lt; 32 === 1` wraparound; `F-072` `Q≤8` sub-cap</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-025</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Bound the entire quantum subsystem and define the MPS approximation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp12, 25, 28; F072, HW08, BUG04; Blueprint pp11, 14, 17; HW08, BUG04, M007</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Q ≤ 10 ... ≤16 KB ... or factorized 2-qubit MPS; F072 Q ≤ 8</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The 16-KiB figure covers one complex-FP64 pure-state vector at Q=10, not temporary gate buffers, saved states, density matrices, or multiple registers/organisms. A factorized product of 2-qubit blocks cannot represent arbitrary entanglement across blocks. A general matrix-product state needs a bond-dimension and truncation contract. F072 locally caps Q at8 while the general cap is10; that may be an intentional stricter limit but is not described.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define representation, workspace, register-count and population-wide byte limits. Specify whether the alternative is independent 2-qubit blocks or an MPS with bounded bond dimension and explicit approximation error. State local versus global cap semantics.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The pure-state vector budget is 16×2^Q bytes, excluding workspace and snapshots. Q≤10 is a hard per-register limit; each workload also declares total register count and total bytes. Tensor-network alternatives declare bond dimensions, permitted entanglement, and truncation errors.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exercise maximum register counts and temporary buffers in the arena budget.; Compare the approximate representation with exact small-Q results, including cross-block entanglement cases.; Generate all local and global cap references from one schema.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`HW16` &amp; `M002`: 32-bit `Math.imul` `Philox4x32-10` implementation avoiding JS `BigInt` heap boxing</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-026</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replace the general O(1) spectral edit check with an explicit bounded guard</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp22, 28, 37; WSE10, ERR02, SetWeight/ScaleBlock; Blueprint pp14–15, 23; ERR02, WSE10, M070</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">O(1) Tier-0 spectral/energy guard; clamp spectral radius ρ(W) ≤ 0.99; Tier-0 (&lt;0.1ms)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A general edit to a variable-sized recurrent matrix cannot be certified by recomputing its spectral radius in O(1) time. The edit itself must at least process its changed entries. A fixed-size operator or incrementally maintained conservative norm bound can be cheap, but the document does not specify one. The &lt;0.1ms claim has no hardware or edit-size bound.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Restrict online operators to a declared maximum edit size; maintain a conservative sufficient bound whose update cost is stated, and schedule larger checks asynchronously. Do not label an approximate spectral estimate an exact certificate.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tier-0 validates ring permissions, finite values, local budgets, and a declared maintained stability bound in O(d) work for d changed entries within a fixed edit cap. Larger edits await shadow/full verification; latency targets require measurement.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Test largest permitted edits and adversarial matrices.; Compare the online bound with an offline reference; record false rejections and prohibit false certification.; Measure p95/p99 validation latency on named hardware.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-08</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`M006` &amp; `HW15`: canonical little-endian `.alifebin` snapshot format (JSON reserved strictly for human metadata manifests)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-027</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Do not treat spectral radius as a universal nonlinear stability guarantee</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp33, 37; G06 and WSE operator table; Blueprint pp12, 23; stability matrix, M070</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ρ(W) ≤ 1 to guarantee non-exploding long-run dynamics; ρ≤0.99</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For a fixed linear recurrence, rho(W)&lt;1 supports asymptotic stability, but rho(W)&lt;=1 alone does not: W=[[1,1],[0,1]] has spectral radius 1 and powers that grow without bound. Even rho&lt;1 does not bound transient amplification: [[0.5,100],[0,0.5]] can amplify a unit state by about 100 in one step. Neither criterion alone certifies the complete nonlinear, time-varying, delayed, input-driven or quantized organism. Orthogonality, spectral norm and spectral radius are distinct constraints.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">State the exact dynamical subsystem and sufficient conditions being certified. Where a contraction is intended, use an appropriate induced-norm/Jacobian bound with activation/input assumptions; assess the complete coupled system separately. Avoid imposing a generic contraction on mechanisms that deliberately require persistent/oscillatory dynamics without redesign.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For a specified fixed linear recurrence, ρ(W)&lt;1 supports asymptotic stability; it is not a general bound on transient or coupled nonlinear dynamics. Online guards certify only their declared subsystem and assumptions.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Include nonnormal, time-varying, delayed, biased, quantized, and saturating examples.; Track transient peak state and constraint violations, not only eigenvalues.; Use system-level perturbation and long-run tests beyond the matrix guard.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-10</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Model 14 &amp; `HW21`: contiguous Governor bands (`&lt;0.70B, 0.70–0.90B, 0.90–1.00B, &gt;1.00B` of `T_frame(Tier)`) + 30-frame hysteresis</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-028</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replace unconditional tiny-diff bounds with size-dependent limits</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp22, 25, 37; WSE08/WSE09, HW06, operator table; Blueprint pp10, 15, 17; HW06, WSE08/WSE09, M006</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Every edit ... &lt;16 KB; Rollback checkpoints ... &lt;64 KB; Merge / Split / Transplant; Requantise</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Arbitrary full-module edits, requantization, topology change, merge/split/transplant, or high-entropy state updates cannot all be represented losslessly below fixed tiny byte caps. Sparse or low-rank encoding only helps when the change has that structure. A compact forward command does not necessarily retain enough previous information for exact inversion.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Set an edit-size limit and an admission policy; allow segmented/full snapshots when necessary. Distinguish forward operator descriptions, reversible before-images, compressed diffs, and complete snapshots. State when a diff may be rejected or promoted to an offline transaction.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Diff size is measured, not guaranteed by codec choice. Online edits are admitted only when their complete rollback journal fits a declared budget; larger changes use bounded segmented/full checkpoints or are rejected before mutation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Try random dense edits and destructive requantization at maximum module sizes.; Verify exact rollback for every admitted edit, including topology and arena metadata.; Force incompressible diffs and prove no silent truncation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Model 7 Mock UI: fixed `Seed: 442 (Practice 100–999)`, `|ΔE|/(E0+Ein): 1.4e-15`, `Band 0 [OK] (8.4ms · 60 FPS vsync)`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-029</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define the thermal/frame governor as a measurable control policy</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp4, 26–28, 31, 40; D2, HW21, ISS02, Model9/12; Blueprint pp4, 10–13, 26, 28; HW21, ISS02, M099, GATE11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">EMA frame time; &gt;90°C; hysteresis; Band0 ...59.5 FPS; Thermal Governor (HW16)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Frame-time EMA measures workload/latency, not temperature itself. The source of the &gt;90°C reading is unspecified. Bands leave the 16.6–18.0ms interval and several boundary equalities undefined; exit thresholds/dwell times for advertised hysteresis are missing. A 59.5-FPS mock panel is inconsistent with the stated Band0 label (&gt;83 FPS) if those values measure the same interval. ISS02 incorrectly points to HW16 (PRNG), not HW21 (governor).</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Distinguish simulation-step, render-frame, and host-temperature metrics; specify optional temperature-provider capability. Fill all threshold intervals and state transitions, downgrade/recovery priorities, dwell times, and overload behavior. Correct ISS02&apos;s gate reference and label mock UI values illustrative.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The governor uses declared metric(s) with complete transition thresholds, hysteresis, dwell times, and fallback behavior. Temperature-based decisions require an available calibrated sensor; otherwise the policy is a frame-time governor. The governor does not promise to hold 60 FPS for unbounded load.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Enumerate boundary values and every state transition, including16.6–18ms.; Inject spikes, sustained overload, unavailable temperature sensors, and recovery oscillation.; Verify displayed band and metric units agree and correct the HW21 cross-reference.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Model 7 Tab 19: `Packed Synapses: 4.8M` (`28B/19B` blocks) + explicit `Gear 0/1/2 amortized ms/frame`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-030</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Label hardware tiers and roofline numbers as targets until measured</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp27–28, 32, 39–40; DIL01/DIL05/ERR01/BUG05, catalog, Models7–9; Blueprint pp9–10, 13–14; tiers, DIL01/DIL05/ERR01/BUG05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">60 FPS; OOP Pointer Chase (&lt;2% peak); ~50ms/tick/creature; 24 GB/s ... violates ... bandwidth physics; 91.4%; 150ms+</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware categories do not determine actual performance. The quoted throughput, latency, OOP peak utilization, finite-element cost, upload-overhead impossibility, and crowd ceilings are not accompanied by reproducible measurements or full assumptions. 24 GB/s is the arithmetic for10^8 FP32 values at60Hz, not a universal hardware impossibility. The 64×64 RGBD×200-agent statement also needs frame rate, bytes/pixel, render complexity, and hardware before a bandwidth conclusion follows.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Retain these as clearly marked illustrative calculations or provisional targets. Add device/runtime/workload/precision/seed metadata and measured distributions before declaring feasibility, infeasibility, or speedup. Separate render FPS from simulated-time throughput; an app can render60FPS while simulation falls behind.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reference tiers are provisional workload targets, not validated guarantees. Publish wall-time per simulated second, render latency, peak RAM/VRAM, event rate, solver iterations, and correctness results for each qualified machine.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reproduce each numeric figure on named hardware or remove its empirical wording.; Include warm-up, duration, p50/p95/p99, power mode, backend, and model fidelity.; Check whole-workload RAM/VRAM fits with reserve; do not certify a tier from weight bytes alone.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-06, B-07, B-17</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 6.2 `HARDWARE_TIERS`: realistic `Tier W` (`8–32×100M` focal or `500×2M` crowd) + `Synaptic Events/s` column; dropped laptop `10^10` claim</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-031</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Make out-of-memory recovery a bounded preflight/restart contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 26 HW24; Blueprint pp11, 26; HW24, M100</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Watchdog catches ... arena &gt;85% ... dumps ... rolls back cleanly</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">An arena watermark can detect planned capacity pressure, but does not guarantee survival of process-wide OOM, runtime termination, GPU device loss, allocation failure outside the arena, or lack of memory to serialize the crash. &apos;Any&apos; nonfinite scan also has a cost unless producers validate as they write. No reserved recovery memory or persistence boundary is specified.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Preflight bounded allocations, reserve emergency metadata buffers, define allocation-failure paths, and use durable checkpoints/process restart for nonrecoverable failures. Distinguish recoverable invalid-state transactions from hard process termination.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The runtime rejects over-budget allocations before mutation and writes bounded diagnostic metadata using reserved resources. Recoverable transactions restore a valid checkpoint; hard OOM/device loss may require restart from the last durable checkpoint.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Inject arena exhaustion, external allocation failure, device loss, disk-full, and abrupt termination.; Verify diagnostics and checkpoints remain valid without allocating an unbounded dump.; Measure nonfinite validation overhead and guarantee all relevant write paths participate.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-11</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 4.1 `MASTER_TABS_19`: concrete `Transport · Refresh Hz · KB/frame` budget on every single tab (`Tab 01–19`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-032</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify bounded telemetry/export backpressure and crash consistency</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp25–27, 31, 40; HW05/HW22, ISS05, export contract, Band3; Blueprint pp10–13; HW05/HW22, ISS05, Band3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">fixed-capacity circular buffers; raw ... stream asynchronously; Pause async Parquet flush</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Fixed ring buffers and pausing disk flush under overload require a policy for data produced while the consumer is slower or stopped. Without one, records are overwritten, simulation blocks, or memory grows; these have different consequences for &apos;total observability&apos; and complete exports. Atomic manifests alone do not establish crash consistency of all dependent chunks.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify per-stream lossless/lossy status, backpressure, sequence numbers, gap reporting, high-water policy, chunk checksums, checkpoint commit order, and disk-full behavior. A telemetry drop must not silently create a complete-looking export.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Every stream declares whether it may drop samples. Lossy rings emit explicit gap records; lossless logging has bounded buffering and a declared pause/stop policy independent of canonical state. Export chunks and manifests use a recoverable commit protocol.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Throttle storage, pause flush, fill rings, and run disk-full/abrupt-stop cases.; Verify sequence gaps are visible and incomplete exports cannot be marked complete.; Confirm memory remains bounded and canonical-state policy remains consistent.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`ISS-05`, `Tab 18`, `HW22`: decoupled overwrite-safe lossy UI telemetry rings (`gap_count`) from backpressure-safe lossless Export rings</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-033</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Hardware, numerical methods, and runtime</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Validate aliasing and event ordering across coarse clocks</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp25, 29, 34, 40; HW10, layered clocks, Model6; Blueprint pp10–12, 17, 21–26; HW10, M005, neural/ecology milestones</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">spikes (5ms); workspace (50ms); viscera (1s); GRN/ecology (60s)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The schedules are proposed without specifying whether events inside an interval are queued, analytically integrated, sampled once, or lost. A fixed timestep can miss short pulses, contact changes, spike coincidences, ordering-sensitive chemistry, and birth/death events. Lower-rate groups need interfaces that preserve causality and exchange budgets, especially when their outputs feed faster subsystems.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define event timestamp resolution, substep/refinement policy, sample/hold/interpolation semantics, simultaneous-event ordering, and conservation of accumulated fluxes. Treat the divider set as an initial configuration subject to error qualification.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Every cross-rate coupling specifies timestamped events or a declared interpolation/flux accumulation rule. Divider settings pass a refinement study and event-detection tests over the expected operating range.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Place pulses and contact changes just before/after each clock boundary.; Compare trajectories and task results with a finer reference schedule.; Test births/deaths, edits, and simultaneous events without duplicated or lost transfers.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`ISS-03` &amp; `HW22`: `.xls`/`.xlsx` auto-sharded `≤500K` rows/sheet while streaming raw tensors to `Parquet`/`HDF5`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-034</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Correct Holm/FDR terminology throughout both PDFs</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 2 (P6), 30, 31 (Tab 13), 41 (10.5), 42 (passes 014–017); Blueprint pp. 24 (M080), 27 (passes 014–017), 28 (GATE-08); contrast familywise wording on p. 14</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Holm-Bonferroni FDR; familywise q ≤ 0.05; FDR q-value</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Holm is a family-wise error control procedure. Mixing its adjusted p-values and alpha with FDR q-values makes the stated pass policy ambiguous. Framework p. 28 uses the correct familywise term, so the document is internally inconsistent.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Select the intended error-control regime once and propagate its name, threshold, UI label, and exported field consistently. For confirmatory gates, retain Holm-adjusted p-values unless a different regime is deliberately chosen.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Within each preregistered confirmatory hypothesis family, apply Holm&apos;s procedure and require adjusted p ≤ α_family. Report raw p, Holm-adjusted p, family ID, and the declared family alpha.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Search both sources and generated PDFs for FDR, q-value, Holm, familywise and alpha.; Use a fixed p-value vector to check the implementation against a reference Holm implementation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A-03, B-15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`THERMODYNAMIC_CONTRACT`: unified 11 conserved pools (`E_patch..E_heat_phys + E_diss_num`) across `P2`, `M004`, `Tab 07` &amp; Sankey</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-035</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replace a universal seed count with an assay-specific power or precision plan</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 2 (P6), 28 (ERR-03), 30, 41 (10.1, 10.5), 42; Blueprint pp. 6 (Model 11), 14 (ERR-03), 24 (M080), 27</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">N ≥ 16 paired fresh seeds; Paired-Seed Power</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A minimum count of 16 does not specify power, target interval width, or the probability of detecting the smallest relevant effect. Binary survival, time-to-event outcomes and continuous accuracy need different plans. Calling the addition &apos;Paired-Seed Power&apos; is unsupported without such a calculation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Keep 16 as an optional minimum screening floor, but determine confirmatory replicate count from pilot variance on practice seeds, meaningful effect, planned analysis and multiplicity. Define an inconclusive state.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">N_confirmatory is chosen before test unblinding from a documented power or precision analysis using practice-only pilot data. The target effect, variance model, alpha, desired power or interval width, stopping rule and handling of deaths are recorded.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Show the calculation or simulation for each assay family.; Confirm power inputs were not estimated from the locked final sample.; Verify underpowered results become inconclusive rather than pass.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A-03, B-12</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unified relative drift tolerance `|ΔE|/(E_0 + E_in,cum) &lt; 1e-12` across `P2`, `CQM05`, `BUG-03`, `M004`, `M090`, `M091`, `GATE-03`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-036</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify a paired effect estimator instead of treating Cliff&apos;s delta as the paired analysis</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 2 (P6), 30, 41 (10.5); Blueprint pp. 6 (Model 11), 24 (M080)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">paired ... Cliff&apos;s δ ≥ 0.33</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The design pairs runs by seed, while ordinary Cliff&apos;s delta describes dominance between sample distributions and does not by itself specify a paired analysis. The fixed .33 threshold also has no task-level justification. This is an incomplete inference contract, not proof that Cliff&apos;s delta can never be reported.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Preregister the experimental unit and paired estimand. Use paired differences, a paired rank effect, or a paired resampling analysis as appropriate. A marginal Cliff&apos;s delta may be secondary if explicitly labeled.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Primary effect: preregistered within-seed intact-minus-control difference with a confidence interval and a task-specific minimum relevant effect. Any supplementary dominance statistic states whether pairing is preserved.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Swap pairings in synthetic data and ensure paired results react appropriately.; Confirm the reported interval, hypothesis test and effect threshold refer to the same estimand.; Do not count creatures or ticks within one evolving world as independent seeds.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-12</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`BUG-03` &amp; `P2`: renamed `FP32 Small-Addend Absorption`; replaced old inaccurate FP64 label with `&apos;Compensated FP64 (&lt; 1e-12)&apos;`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-037</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Declare multiplicity across contrasts, suites and repeated promotions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 30, section 5.1 promotion rules; pp. 6–7 roll-ups; Blueprint p. 24 (M080), p. 26 (M100), p. 28 release gates</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">within each checklist family; previously locked floors</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The family contains multiple gates and usually three intact-versus-control contrasts, but its exact members are unstated. Nine separately controlled families do not automatically yield the same global error guarantee. Repeated promotion attempts also require a declared sequential or repeated-testing policy; the separate holdout-lifecycle repair addresses test-set exposure.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">List hypotheses/contrasts and family boundaries before evaluation; explicitly state whether guarantees are per family or across the whole release. Specify how repeated evaluation and promotion attempts affect the testing policy and error guarantee.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Each confirmatory family has an immutable hypothesis manifest. The release states its per-family or global error guarantee. Once a locked sample is inspected for iteration it becomes a regression sample; final certification uses a fresh concealed sample under a preregistered stopping rule.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Enumerate every p-value entering every correction.; Check roll-ups are not counted as independent new evidence.; Audit seed exposure and promotion-attempt logs.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">E-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`THERMODYNAMIC_CONTRACT` &amp; `P2`: explicit Second-Law one-way sink rule (`dE_heat_phys/dt ≥ 0`, `0` heat-to-ATP backflow)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-038</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate non-regression from two-sided equivalence and define margins</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 2 (P6), 28 (ERR-03), 30, 41 (10.5); Blueprint p. 14 (ERR-03)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">TOST floor; TOST equivalence non-regression</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">TOST establishes equivalence inside two prespecified bounds; preserving a lower performance floor is generally a one-sided non-inferiority question. Without numerical margins, direction, outcome scale and confidence level, neither instruction is executable. A large improvement can fail a narrow two-sided equivalence test despite satisfying non-regression.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Choose non-inferiority for &apos;no meaningful degradation&apos; and reserve TOST for outcomes where both upward and downward deviations matter. Set justified margins before observing results.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For performance floors, require the lower confidence bound of the paired new-minus-reference effect to exceed −Δ_loss. For equivalence, preregister [−Δ_lower,+Δ_upper] and apply the selected TOST procedure.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Include a large-improvement example, a small tolerated loss, a harmful loss and an inconclusive result.; Verify all margins have units and a substantive rationale.; Apply the planned family-level correction where needed.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A-03, B-15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`SOFT06`, `BUG-02`, `M022`: XPBD numerical damping booked to `E_diss_num ≥ 0` (`ΔE_diss_num &lt; -1e-15` faults against free energy)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-039</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Restore the distinction among HOT-1, HOT-2 and HOT-3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 6, HOT-1–HOT-3; Blueprint p. 15, HOT-1–HOT-3; p. 23 (M064)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">HOT-1 ... discriminate clean signal from internal noise; HOT-3 Acting on confidence</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The attributed indicator mapping moves monitoring into HOT-1 and reduces HOT-3 to confidence-responsive behavior. In the source, HOT-1 concerns generative/top-down/noisy perception; HOT-2 concerns reliability monitoring; HOT-3 additionally concerns general belief formation/action selection and reliance on monitoring in belief revision. An opt-out reflex alone therefore does not establish the full cited HOT-3.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate the source indicator from the project&apos;s assay. Add a belief-update/general-action-consumer assay to HOT-3 and keep calibration as supporting evidence rather than the complete definition.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">HOT-1: qualifying first-order perception architecture. HOT-2: second-order reliability monitoring. HOT-3: monitored representations inform a general belief/action system and systematically guide its belief updates.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Give HOT-1, HOT-2 and HOT-3 separate fixtures.; Include a confidence-triggered opt-out system that passes the local behavior check but fails the full HOT-3 gate.; Trace monitoring outputs into belief revision and downstream decisions.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-08</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`D4`, `SOFT21`, `M011`, Model 10: Gánti Chemoton explicitly couples `A`, membrane `T`, and template polymer `pV_n` (`n ≥ N*`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-040</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Restore omitted GWT-4 and HOT-4 requirements</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 6, GWT-4 and HOT-4; Blueprint p. 15, GWT-4 and HOT-4</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GWT-4 State-dependent attention; HOT-4 Quality space</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The GWT-4 paraphrase drops sequential module querying for complex tasks. HOT-4&apos;s generic similarity manifold drops sparse, smooth coding. The document may choose a simplified local target, but cannot report full fidelity to the source indicator on these narrower descriptions.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add the omitted clauses to the canonical indicator record and use separate tests for sequential querying, coding sparsity and local representational smoothness.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GWT-4 includes state-dependent sequential workspace queries across specialists. HOT-4 includes sparse, smooth coding producing a structured quality space.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Demonstrate a multistep task requiring successive specialist queries.; Measure sparsity and test smooth interpolation/generalization under controlled perturbations.; Check both PDF tables and the shared gate schema.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`SOFT22` &amp; `M012`: Michaelis-Menten saturation + allosteric `[ATP]` inhibition integrated via bounded implicit substep</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-041</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Recommendation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Label project-specific indicator assays as operationalizations, not verbatim definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 6, GWT-2, AST-1, PP-1, AE-1–AE-2; Blueprint p. 15 corresponding rows</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">attentional blink; peers&apos; gaze; precision-weighted prediction errors; efference copy</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Several rows add a particular implementation or assay, while AE-2 is narrowed to self/external discrimination. These are defensible design choices, but passing or failing one chosen implementation is not automatically equivalent to satisfying or lacking the more general literature indicator.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Store source_definition, local_operationalization, rationale and evidence separately. Include alternate realizations when the source allows them. Explicitly test flexible competing goals for AE-1 and use of the output–input model for AE-2.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">These are project-specific operational tests inspired by the cited indicators; extensions such as social gaze and attentional-blink tasks are reported separately.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Review the source-to-assay mapping row by row.; Ensure optional extensions cannot silently change the meaning of the base indicator.; Retain the existing statement that indicator passes do not prove subjective consciousness.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">H-08</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`M004` &amp; `GATE-04`: added subtle `-2e-11` near-threshold enthalpy leak mutant alongside gross `-1e-5` leak test</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-042</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Clarify the relationship between the PCI-inspired gate and IIT</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 7 (F2), p. 8 (CQM21), p. 28 (ERR-06), p. 29 (Stage 7); Blueprint p. 15 (F2), p. 16 (CQM21), p. 24 (M078)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Integrated Information / PCI</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The heading Integrated Information / PCI places a theory-related concept and an empirical perturbation-complexity measure under one label. The text does not explicitly claim to calculate IIT Phi, but the label leaves that distinction unclear. A simulated PCI-inspired statistic needs its own definition and validation domain and should not be treated as an IIT calculation by implication.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Rename or annotate F2 as a perturbational-complexity/causal-response assay, explicitly distinguishing it from an IIT-specific quantity. If an IIT calculation is intended, specify it separately. Preserve the existing neutral consciousness disclaimer.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F2: Perturbational response complexity (PCI-inspired simulation assay). Reports distributed causal response and differentiation under defined controls; does not compute IIT Φ or certify consciousness.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verify all labels, exports and charts distinguish PCI-like statistics from Φ.; Document the estimator and its simulation-specific validation.; Keep consciousness claims outside the numerical score.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`M083` &amp; `L032-A4`: Kleiber allometry specified as statistical power-law `Slope ∈ [0.70, 0.80], R² ≥ 0.95` over `≥8×` mass</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-043</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify a reproducible PCI-inspired estimator and defeat noise/rate confounds</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 7 (F2), 28 (ERR-06), 29 (Stage 7); Blueprint p. 24 (M078)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Cortical pulse Lempel-Ziv spatiotemporal complexity; PCI(Wake) &gt; PCI(NREM-SWS) and PCI(Lesion)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Lempel-Ziv complexity by itself can reward irrelevant stochastic variation. The gate omits pulse location/size, response window, baseline subtraction or significance, binning, normalization, trial count, channel ordering and population-size comparability. Hardcoding low-activity sleep can manufacture the proposed wake-versus-sleep and wake-versus-lesion differences. The document does not require a sleep-versus-lesion ordering.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define a versioned estimator and independently designed controls. Measure reproducible perturbation-evoked responses, not arbitrary spontaneous noise, and compare activity-matched intact, disconnected, noise-driven and stereotyped networks.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compute a preregistered normalized complexity measure on baseline-controlled perturbation responses. Report response spread and differentiation separately. Wake/sleep/lesion ordering is a test hypothesis, not a built-in state label.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A disconnected random-noise network must not qualify as integrated solely from high raw complexity.; Test sensitivity to firing rate, network size, channel permutation, pulse amplitude and bin width.; Publish raw trial responses and estimator version.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`SOFT24/25` &amp; `M016`: osmotic Donnan balance &amp; Cori cycle bounded within viable physiological `[Osm, pH]` envelopes</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-044</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Clarify heterogeneous framework categories and dependent rollups</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 7 (F1–F10), p. 6 aggregate rule; Blueprint p. 15 (6B), p. 24 (M080)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">10 Consciousness Frameworks; F10 Butlin 14 roll-up; Full 24-check gate</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F1–F10 combine theories, architectures, proposed mechanisms and task capacities. The Framework correctly identifies F10 as a composite and removes that designated rollup from one administrative tally; preserve that distinction. The remaining categories and evidence dependencies need explicit typing so downstream scoreboards do not misread coverage counts as independent confirmations or a consciousness probability.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Classify each item by type and record its dependencies and supported local claim. Preserve the existing F10 rollup rule and neutral disclaimer. For named theories such as F8 and F9, link the source and the selected mathematical realization; identify what the unspecified field model in F5 actually implements.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F1–F9 are heterogeneous project mechanism/framework assays. F10 is a dependent conjunction roll-up. Suite totals describe project coverage and are not a calibrated probability or independent evidence count.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Check that parent/child gates cannot be double-counted as independent discoveries.; Require a source, equation/model contract and test for each named framework.; Ensure release language matches the modest claim supported by each assay.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`SOFT26` &amp; `M029`: Arrhenius `Q10` paired with high-temperature denaturation drop above `T_max`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-045</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify the paradigm behind the lag-1 attentional-blink target</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Blueprint p. 23 (M061); related Framework p. 6 GWT-2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">producing lag-1 attentional blink</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The row requires a lag-1 attentional blink without specifying the stimulus and task paradigm. Canonical rapid-serial visual-presentation experiments commonly show lag-1 sparing and a later short-lag impairment, but sparing is not universal. A lag-1 impairment is therefore not intrinsically impossible; it needs a matching paradigm and should not be presented as a generic hallmark of a workspace.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify the rapid-serial task, stimulus durations, target/distractor sequence, lag definition and accuracy conditional on first-target detection. State the expected full lag-response curve for that chosen paradigm, including sparing if predicted, instead of requiring an unqualified lag-1 deficit.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Measure an attentional-blink curve across target lags in the declared paradigm. Preregister whether lag-1 sparing or impairment is expected and why; compare with a matched single-target baseline. No universal lag-1 deficit is assumed.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Plot the whole lag-response curve rather than a single lag.; Keep stimulus masking and task-switch effects distinguishable.; Verify that the workspace mechanism, rather than a scripted refractory counter alone, explains the intended claim.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`SOFT29`, `N49`, `M086`: cryptobiotic vitrification `&lt;2%` basal burn + `≥90%` rehydration revival gate</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-046</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Declare the scope of Michaelis–Menten kinetics as a chosen approximation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 24 (SOFT22), p. 38 (Saturating Flux); Blueprint p. 8 (SOFT22), p. 18 (M012)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">All metabolic reaction fluxes obey v = V_max [S] / (K_m + [S])</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A synthetic model may deliberately use saturating Michaelis–Menten-style fluxes. The text needs to distinguish that choice from a universal biochemical law and from the separate requirements for stoichiometric closure, nonnegative pools and thermodynamic directionality. Saturation alone does not establish those properties; a linear rate law does not by itself imply an infinite reaction rate.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Declare the simplified reaction network and rate law per reaction. Use Michaelis–Menten where intended; retain explicit transport, mass-action, reversible or cooperative laws when the model requires them. Enforce substrate-limited integration independently.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Selected enzyme-mediated fluxes use bounded Michaelis–Menten approximations under declared assumptions; each reaction specifies substrates, products, stoichiometry, units, reversibility and numerical positivity constraints.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verify every reaction&apos;s units and conserved moieties.; Stress-test zero substrate, high substrate, product accumulation and depletion within a large time step.; Do not design the linear control to &apos;blow up&apos; by omitting basic substrate availability.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-07</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`F-031`, `M014`, `CIT-05`: antithetic motif specifies `z_1, z_2 ≥ 0`, positive-orthant dual actuation &amp; zero-dilution `γ=0` condition</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-047</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Qualify antithetic perfect-adaptation guarantees</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 10 (F-030–F-031), p. 38 antithetic equations; Blueprint p. 18 (M014)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">guarantees zero steady-state glycemic error; Zero drift clamp</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Perfect adaptation depends on properties of the coupled plant/controller and the existence of the relevant stable or stationary regime. The two controller equations alone do not prove stability, feasibility under starvation/saturation, or exact tracking of a moving circadian target. Finite arithmetic adds an implementation tolerance.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">State the operating envelope, control authority, conservation cost, stability/ergodicity assumptions, and distinction between steady-state constant-target tracking and dynamic tracking. Include anti-windup or a defined saturation response.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For feasible constant targets in the verified stable operating regime, the controller converges to the target within declared numerical/statistical tolerance. Moving targets and resource saturation use separately bounded tracking-error criteria.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Check steady disturbances and parameter variation across the declared domain.; Include actuator saturation, depleted fuel, delays and moving setpoints.; Show that controller species/costs obey the same bounded pool rules.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`SOFT01` &amp; `M021`: hydrostatic volume constraint written quantitatively as `|ΔV|/V_0 &lt; 0.01` (`1%`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-048</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate energy-ledger accuracy from physically consistent XPBD coupling</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 2 (P2), p. 23 (SOFT06), p. 38 (First-Law Coupling), p. 40; Blueprint pp. 6–7 (Model 3, SOFT06), p. 17 first-law milestones, p. 19 soft mechanics</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exact First-Law Thermodynamics; First-Law exact; bit-exact FP64 energy conservation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compensated FP64 accounting reduces summation error but cannot make an arbitrary dynamics update thermodynamically correct. XPBD&apos;s implicit stepping introduces mechanical dissipation. Updating a heat bucket to force closure can conceal numerical error unless modeled dissipation and solver residuals are separated.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Declare mechanical, chemical, kinetic, thermal and boundary-flux budgets; compute work consistently with the chosen solver; report numerical residual separately from physical heat. Replace &apos;exact thermodynamics&apos; with a measurable accounting/integration contract.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Energy accounting closes within a declared tolerance. Physical dissipation, active work, stored energy and numerical solver residuals are independently tracked; conservation alone is not a claim of biological or thermodynamic fidelity.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Run free-oscillator, damped-oscillator, actuator-work and collision benchmarks across decreasing step sizes.; Show no unmodeled energy source under cyclic actuation.; Confirm numerical losses are not silently relabeled as biological heat.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A-03, B-15</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 5 &amp; `SOFT06`: coupled XPBD elastic potential `E_elastic`, active muscle `ATP -&gt; Work + E_heat_phys`, and `E_diss_num ≥ 0`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-049</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Distinguish Q10 scaling from an Arrhenius rate law</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 24 (SOFT26), p. 38 (Saturating Flux); Blueprint p. 8 (SOFT26)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Arrhenius Q10 thermal rate scaling</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A constant Q10 multiplier over a chosen temperature range is not the same model as an Arrhenius law with activation energy. &apos;Scale lawfully&apos; does not specify which, its temperature units/range, or the denaturation response. Either simplified model is a legitimate design choice.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Name the selected approximation and define T reference, valid interval, units and behavior beyond it; separate thermal enzyme inactivation from simple rate acceleration.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Use k(T)=k_ref Q10^((T−T_ref)/10°C) over the declared interval, with a separately defined denaturation/inactivation model; or specify the Arrhenius alternative and its parameters.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Check unit conversion and continuity at boundaries.; Verify rates stay finite and pool updates remain positive.; Do not infer universal biological validity from fitting the chosen approximation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">H-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`M024` (Stage-3 0-brain passive terrain stability) cleanly separated from `M044/SOFT08` (Stage-4 `≥50%` spike/m offload at equal speed/fail)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-050</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate lactate, proton balance and fatigue instead of one causal bucket</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 24 (SOFT25); Blueprint p. 8 (SOFT25), p. 18 (M016)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">H+/lactate, fatiguing muscle contractility until cleared via respiration/Cori cycle</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The shorthand can be read as lactate production directly causing the acid load and as Cori cycling directly clearing all acid/fatigue. That is not an adequate reaction-level proton/redox model. A simple abstract fatigue proxy is allowed, but should be labeled as such rather than presented as biochemical closure.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Either document explicit stoichiometry, proton buffering, transport and ATP/redox coupling, or rename this a phenomenological exercise-fatigue pool with a stated relationship to simulated lactate.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Track lactate and acid-base state separately. Proton production/consumption, buffering and transport follow the chosen reaction model; fatigue depends on declared physiological proxies rather than lactate concentration alone.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Check reaction proton and charge balances at the modeled pH convention.; Test high lactate without automatically asserting the same acidification.; Document which aspects are mechanistic and which are fitted abstractions.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-01, D-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 7.1 `BUTLIN_14`: aligned `GWT-4` (top-down context modulation) and `HOT-1..4` (reality monitoring, meta-belief, confidence agency, quality space)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-051</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Correct the Baldwin-effect label on within-life organ remodeling</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 10 (F-034); related p. 17 life-cycle rows and p. 20 (N41); Blueprint p. 25 (M090)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Load-driven organ plasticity (Baldwin)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Workload-induced hypertrophy/atrophy within one life is phenotypic plasticity. The Baldwin effect concerns how learning/plasticity changes evolutionary selection across generations; direct transmission of acquired weights is a different inheritance mechanism.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Rename F-034 to load-dependent phenotypic remodeling. For M090 define a separate multigenerational Baldwin experiment, distinguishing selection on inherited propensity from direct somatic-to-germline writeback.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F-034: Load-dependent tissue plasticity. Baldwin assay: compare evolutionary change with versus without within-life learning while keeping the declared inheritance channel fixed.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Track genotype, phenotype and learned-state inheritance separately.; Include a no-learning lineage control.; Do not count direct transfer of learned weights as evidence of Baldwinian selection.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`GWT-2` &amp; `M061`: attentional blink specified at **lags 2–5 (`100–500 ms`) with lag-1 sparing (`|Lag1-Lag8| ≤ 10%`)**</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-052</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Make the 3/4 allometric exponent an explicit model hypothesis</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 3 category 9 and p. 17 life-history catalogue; Blueprint p. 25 (M083)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Kleiber M^0.75; Linear M^1.0 burn control overheats/starves large adults</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The 3/4 exponent is not a universal constraint for every organism, size range and metabolic state. The linear-control outcome is also not inevitable: it depends on coefficients, intake and heat exchange. The proposed organism may choose .75, but passing that imposed formula does not validate biological allometry.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Use B=aM^b with declared units/domain and justify or estimate b. Separate formula implementation from an evolutionary or empirical test of scaling. Do not prerequire a particular adverse result from the comparison exponent.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Baseline model uses b=0.75 as a declared hypothesis. Evaluate sensitivity to b and normalization; compare energy balance and performance across the target mass range.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Keep reference metabolic rate equal at a declared reference mass when comparing exponents.; Run a mass-range sensitivity study.; Report fitted or assumed exponents explicitly.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`F2` &amp; `M078`: renamed `Integrated Information Motivation / Forked-Snapshot PCI*` (no exact `O(2^N)` IIT `Φ` claim)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-053</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Recommendation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Mark biological mechanism hypotheses and species-specific abstractions as such</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 10–13, especially F-032, F-039–F-047, F-067, F-081–F-084, F-098–F-099; Blueprint pp. 18 (M013/M015/M020), 21 (M046/M049/M050), 24 (M071/M072), 25 (M088)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">requires slow glial calcium; exploits left/novelty right; pruning runs exclusively in NREM</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The catalogue mixes chosen synthetic mechanisms, species-specific biology and hypothesis-derived interpretations, often in universal present tense. For example, global sleep downscaling is a hypothesis-inspired rule, and exclusive NREM pruning or universal left-exploit/right-novelty division should not be presented as established general biology. Mixing taxa is permissible for an original synthetic creature.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add provenance and status per mechanism: measured biological observation, theory/hypothesis, engineering analogue, or invented in-world feature. Replace universal wording with the scope actually supported or with &apos;this model implements&apos;.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Each catalogue row states biological source/scope and the deliberate simplification used in this creature. Biological inspiration does not imply that all listed mechanisms coexist naturally or are necessary for life.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Require one source or an explicit invented-model label for each biological attribution.; Check causal tests validate the implemented rule, not a universal claim about real organisms.; Preserve creative synthetic combinations rather than removing them merely for being unfamiliar.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`M078`: defines deterministic forked-snapshot perturbation protocol for `PCI*` without corrupting live trajectory state</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-054</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define the state manifold and parameter map before claiming a quantum natural gradient</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 18 (N02/N04/N08), p. 33 geometry catalogue; Blueprint p. 24 (M076)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Synaptic updates preconditioned by the quantum geometric tensor</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A QGT belongs to a specified parameterized quantum-state family. An arbitrary neural weight vector has no uniquely defined QGT. Merely using a metric or curvature penalty does not make an update a quantum natural gradient, and reduced overshoot is a hypothesis rather than a guarantee.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify θ→|ψ(θ)&gt;, normalization, gauge treatment, the real metric, singular-metric regularization and how neural parameters are mapped to it. Otherwise use the accurate label classical metric-preconditioned plasticity.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For the declared parameterized state model, update with the regularized Fubini–Study metric and a bounded step; compare against a budget-matched classical optimizer. The quantum-inspired label describes the mathematics, not a demonstrated quantum advantage.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Verify metric symmetry/positive semidefiniteness and gauge invariance where applicable.; Test singular and near-singular states.; Match optimization budgets and report failures as well as gains.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`F10`: reframed as `Butlin-14 Indicator Profile Scorecard` reporting per-theory family vectors + optional `14/14` badge</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-055</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Give topological moods and geometric surprise actual mathematical contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 18 (N01–N04, N08–N10); Blueprint p. 24 (M074/M076)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">integer winding/Chern-like invariants; mood flips only at genuine phase transitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Winding numbers, Chern numbers, curvature and Fubini–Study distance require specified domains, metrics, states and boundary conditions. &apos;Chern-like&apos; is not enough to compute or test a quantity. An integer-valued mood counter is not automatically a topological invariant, and a designer-selected transition is not automatically a physical phase transition.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define the state space, closed loop/surface, estimator and invariance class for each feature; otherwise rename to an ordinary discrete-state or geometric-control mechanism. Define how elapsed time is decoded despite phase periodicity for N01.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Topological/geometric features are optional model hypotheses with a versioned mathematical definition and controls showing invariance or sensitivity under the declared perturbations.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Test coordinate/gauge transformations that should leave the measure unchanged.; Test state noise and boundary crossings that should or should not change the invariant.; Compare with a simple finite-state or Euclidean control at equal compute cost.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`CQM02`: operationalized open-endedness via held-out niche competence &amp; interaction growth exceeding neutral-drift controls</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-056</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replace claims to recognize pure or quantum randomness with bounded predictability tests</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 18 (N05), p. 20 (N50), p. 21 (N87), p. 12 (F-068/F-072); Blueprint p. 23 (M069)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Distinguishes quantum/unpredictable noise ... PRNG-patterned; identifies pure-noise world channels</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Finite observations do not generally identify a channel&apos;s physical randomness origin or prove that it is unlearnable. A classically simulated qubit is not an independent hardware quantum entropy source. Tests can detect particular patterns or establish lack of useful predictive gain within a specified budget.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reframe the capability as calibrated predictability/learnability assessment over named generator classes, horizons and budgets. Distinguish seeded PRNG, replayed external entropy and simulated quantum-state sampling in manifests.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Detects exploitable structure in specified test families and reduces exploration when held-out predictive gain is below a declared threshold; makes no universal claim to certify randomness or its physical origin.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Include a strong PRNG, weak patterned generators and genuine/replayed entropy where available.; Blind generator identity and prevent access to seeds.; Match output distributions and sampling budgets in N50.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`F-084`: corrected to `~2/3 of total octopus neurons` in brachial arm cords (Hochner 2012); `F-083` `2%` SDR vs `5–10%` fly Kenyon</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-057</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Do not infer signal honesty solely from paying ATP</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 20 (N59), p. 2 (P8), p. 8 (CQM14); Blueprint p. 16 (CQM14), p. 25 (M089)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">strict proportional ATP so weak bluffers cannot fake high physiological condition</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A positive signal cost does not by itself make deception impossible or establish an honest equilibrium. Honest signaling depends on how costs and benefits vary with condition and on receiver responses. Resource bookkeeping is necessary for this model but is not the whole game-theoretic result.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify condition-dependent feasibility/costs, sender and receiver payoffs, and opportunity costs. Test dishonest mutants and alternative receiver policies instead of requiring honesty by label.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Signals incur declared costs; an honesty claim requires condition-predictive signaling and resistance to profitable deceptive strategies under the defined ecology.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Attempt low-condition high-signal and high-condition low-signal strategies.; Measure receiver benefit and sender invasion advantage.; Separate physically unfakeable index cues from costly signals.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`GW06`, `WSE10`, `WSE-OP01`: Tier-0 stability guard uses contractive induced norm `‖W‖_2 ≤ 0.99` (bounding both transient &amp; `ρ(W)`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-058</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Test the claimed Markov-blanket partition instead of equating it with a membrane or swarm boundary</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 8 (CQM15), p. 20 (N56); Blueprint p. 16 (CQM15), p. 24 (M079)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">membrane/cuticle + Markov blanket; conditionally autonomous collective swarm boundary</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A physical shell or compact swarm does not alone define the required statistical conditional-independence structure. The proposed labels need named internal, external, sensory and active variables, a time resolution and a model distribution.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify the probabilistic/dynamical partition and distinguish exact structural separation from approximate empirical conditional independence. Avoid inferring consciousness or organismhood merely from the partition.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">For the declared state partition and observation scale, assess conditional dependence of internal and external variables given the proposed blanket; report model assumptions and residual dependence.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Check prohibited direct information paths in the model.; Estimate residual dependence with a declared method and sufficient data.; Compare the true partition with shuffled or deliberately leaky partitions.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`WSE09` &amp; `N15`: declared 3 explicit rollback domains (`D_creature`, `D_coupled_patch`, `D_full_sim`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-059</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Recommendation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate invented alien-biology features from empirical claims and give each a transfer protocol</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 18–21, especially N15/N24/N29/N32/N34/N38/N41–N42/N49/N51–N52/N96–N97; Blueprint pp. 22 (M060), 24 (M074–M077), 25 (M086/M088)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">shared contact dreaming; dream-seeded germline; fidelity touch; neural graft</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">These can be coherent in-world engineering ideas, but names alone do not define a mechanism or supply evidence for corresponding natural biology. Direct exchange of memory or internal state can also bypass the declared sensory/learning challenge unless the channel is explicit.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Retain the ideas as optional inventions/hypotheses. For each declare transmitted variables, bandwidth/range/noise, energy and mass cost, inheritance/identity semantics and the task claim. Label extensions distinctly from biology-backed core requirements.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Frontier features are explicit synthetic extensions. Their implementation and functional value are tested within the declared world; a pass does not establish their existence or necessity in natural organisms.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Audit all inter-agent and cross-generation data channels.; Ensure comparator agents receive matched communication/compute budgets.; Test causal function and cost without claiming real grief, dreaming or sentience from a display label.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-10</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`F-068/072/092`, `L040`, `N01..08`, `N50`: mandatory parameter- &amp; FLOP-matched classical stochastic/oscillator baselines</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-060</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Remove guaranteed noise shrinkage from the NoisyNet row</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 12 (F-069)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">noise scales automatically shrink as local task uncertainty resolves</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">NoisyNet learns noise parameters with the task objective; the architecture does not guarantee monotonically shrinking noise or identify those scales with calibrated epistemic uncertainty.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Describe trainable noise honestly and test any desired uncertainty coupling separately.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Learnable parametric weight noise supports exploration; its learned magnitude and relationship to task uncertainty are measured rather than assumed.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Plot noise parameters and held-out calibration over learning.; Allow increases when a changed environment benefits from renewed exploration.; Compare against fixed-noise and action-noise baselines.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">B-04, K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`WSE06` (`CSR` delta overlay for procedural blocks) &amp; reconciled `&lt;16 KB` single `WSE` diff (`WSE08`) vs `&lt;64 KB` creature checkpoint (`HW06`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-061</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Scope the claimed optimality of Lévy foraging</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 12 (F-070)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">μ ≈ 2 maximizes sparse patch discovery when local gradients vanish</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The cited inverse-square result depends on a particular search problem, including sparse revisitable targets. Absent local gradients alone does not establish that the same exponent is optimal under mortality, travel energy, boundaries, target depletion or memory.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">State a truncated Lévy search policy as a candidate and specify the ecological assumptions for any optimality claim.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tests μ near 2 as a candidate sparse-search strategy under declared target renewal, sensing, movement-cost and boundary conditions; selects or reports the best policy empirically within that domain.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compare Brownian, ballistic, correlated and memory-guided controls.; Vary patch density, renewal and cost.; Include truncation lengths and finite-world effects in the specification.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">G-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 4.7 `evaluateCubeCell(x,y,z)`: genuine 3D compatibility rules incorporating `Z4` hyperbolic, `Z5` bump, `Z6` steerable, `Z7` SDR, `Z9` S2S</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-062</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define the target distribution and numerical conditions for Langevin sampling</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 12 (F-071)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Stochastic membrane trajectories sample directly from the posterior distribution</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Noise plus recurrent motion is not enough to establish posterior sampling. The target density, drift/score, diffusion, boundaries, discretization and convergence/mixing conditions are absent. Quantization and finite steps can introduce bias.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Provide the stochastic dynamics and inference target; use &apos;approximate sampling&apos; unless exactness conditions are established.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Implements a declared Langevin-style sampler for a specified latent posterior, with measured discretization bias, mixing and approximation error under the actual precision and time-step budget.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Recover moments on analytically solvable targets.; Check multiple modes, autocorrelation and effective sample size.; Perform step-size/precision convergence tests and compare with a trusted reference sampler.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">G-02, G-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Model 9 Panel B, `Z0–Z9` tables, and `ALife-Hybrid-Cube-10x10x10.csv/.xlsx` all generated from `evaluateCubeCell` (100% synced)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-063</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Distinguish neural branching criticality from ecological coexistence and qualify optimality</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 13 (F-096), p. 21 (N78); Blueprint p. 26 (M091 and related biosphere gates)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">branching σ ≈ 1 ... max dynamic range; Three-guild trophic criticality ... self-stabilize</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A branching statistic near one does not alone establish criticality or optimal computation in every neural model. Stable coexistence of three ecological guilds is a different property and is not a definition of criticality. Nonextinction also needs a finite horizon and perturbation envelope.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify neural criticality estimators and finite-size/control comparisons; rename the ecological gate to bounded trophic coexistence unless a genuine ecological phase-transition analysis is added.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F-096 tests the relationship among the chosen branching estimate, response dynamic range and robustness. N78 tests coexistence and recovery over a declared horizon; criticality is a separate optional hypothesis.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compare subcritical, near-critical and supercritical settings at matched input statistics.; Check estimator bias and finite-size effects.; Run biosphere disturbances and report extinction probabilities.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">G-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Grayscale-safe printable text badges `[OK]`, `[OVER]`, `[ADAPT]`, `[EXCL]` across all Cube slices, Model 9, and spreadsheets</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-064</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Science, statistics, and operational definitions</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Do not equate reconstruction error or precision with calibrated metacognition</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 10 (F-024/F-029), p. 13 (F-077), p. 21 (N81/N83/N86); Blueprint p. 23 (M064), p. 24 (M074)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">reconstruction residual serves directly as second-order uncertainty signal</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A residual can be a useful input, but it is not automatically epistemic uncertainty, calibrated confidence or a second-order model of one&apos;s own reliability. Noisy but familiar inputs can have large residuals; a confidently wrong model can have small residuals.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define the predicted event and probability target, distinguish measurement noise from model uncertainty, and validate confidence by calibration and decision value on held-out shifts.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Residuals and precision estimates are candidate features for a learned or specified reliability monitor. Metacognitive claims require calibration and useful selective decisions beyond raw error magnitude.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Use separate noise-shift and knowledge-shift tests.; Report calibration, discrimination, coverage-versus-risk and decision utility.; Compare against a residual-threshold baseline and verify the monitor uses no outcome oracle.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">G-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 4.7: renamed `&apos;Five Proposed Reference Starter Configurations&apos;` (replacing unsupported optimality claim)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-065</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Contradiction</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Resolve forward dependencies in the strict bottom-up gate order</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 29, §5.1 Stages 2–4; p. 43 Steps 2–4; Blueprint p. 18 M015/M017/M018; p. 19 M024; p. 20 M034; p. 21 M041/M047</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Stage 3 ... ≥50% morphological spike offload; Stage 4 Spiking/sLTC Core</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The strict order requires a reduction in brain spikes before the spiking brain exists. Earlier visceral milestones also require foraging, learned taste aversion, and learned rejection of decoys before their sensory and learning dependencies are built. The early five-mutant pass is similarly ambiguous before the corresponding mechanisms exist.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate component conformance from integrated behavioral acceptance. Publish a dependency DAG; allow test fixtures early, but defer the organism-level capability pass until all prerequisites exist.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Stages establish components in dependency order. Early stages may pass component tests with declared fixtures. M017/M018 behavioral acceptance requires sensory and plasticity milestones; M024 spike-offload acceptance requires a working spiking controller. Fixture passes do not count as integrated organism passes.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Topologically sort declared milestone dependencies and reject cycles or references to unavailable components.; Verify no spike-offload pass can be recorded before M041 and its matched controller baseline pass.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">PDF-1 &amp; PDF-2: visual SVG Models `1..14` placed/indexed in strict ascending numerical order + exact `Z0–Z9` page/pair labels</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-066</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Contradiction</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unify stage, phase, layer and milestone mappings</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 29 §5.1 Stage 0 M001–M003 and Stage 1 M004–M010; Blueprint p. 1 layer stack; p. 17 Phase 1/L0 M001–M010</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Stage 0 Preregistration &amp; Repo Health M001–M003</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The Framework maps M001–M003 to preregistration/repo health, but Blueprint M001 and M002 are the SoA arena and PRNG. Its Layer/Stage/Phase labels are not one interchangeable index. The Framework&apos;s Stage 0 exit also names repository, unit and hardware declarations not completely represented by these three milestones.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Generate a crosswalk with distinct layer_id, build_phase_id, stage_gate_id and prerequisite IDs. Either move preregistration outside M001–M100 or explicitly remap the milestones; update both documents from that mapping.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Layer L0 contains M001–M010. Preregistration gate S0 is completed before L0 and records repository, units, seed policy and hardware profile. Build phases and release stages refer to the canonical milestone crosswalk rather than sharing an implicit numbering scheme.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compare every M001–M100 mapping in both PDFs against the canonical crosswalk.; Confirm all stage entry/exit obligations have explicit requirement IDs.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">H-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`buildAll825ExecutableChecks()`: assigned canonical machine IDs `LIFE-L001-A1 .. LIFE-L100-A5` to all 500 `LIFE-100` checkboxes</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-067</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Treat the Day-1/Week-4 schedule as an unvalidated illustration</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 43 §11.3; Blueprint p. 1 Solo-Builder Day-1 MVO sequence</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Step 3 (Day 3–5); Step 5 (Week 3); Step 6 (Week 4+)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The calendar estimates span new soft-body physics, metabolism, neural learning, workspace, protected self-editing, 19 tabs, ontogeny and ecology with no measured implementation effort. The Blueprint&apos;s Day-1 minimum includes seven substantial subsystems. It is an order of work, not an evidenced duration estimate.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Remove fixed day/week durations or label them examples with assumptions and uncertainty. Define a smaller first demonstrator and a separate full-research roadmap.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Illustrative build order; durations are unestimated until a prototype establishes effort and throughput. First demonstrator: one creature, one environment, bounded resource accounting, local sensing, a small controller, one learning task and replayable evidence. Additional modules enter after a specific experiment requires them.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">No remaining timeline is worded as a delivery guarantee.; The first demonstrator has an explicit acceptance test and excludes requirements assigned to later profiles.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">H-05, K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Every single one of the `825` items carries structured `primary_contrast`, `control_arms`, `min_paired_seeds`, and `stat_gate`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-068</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replace &apos;820 unique mechanisms&apos; with an auditable overlap graph</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 6 de-duplicated aggregate rule; pp10–11 F-024/F-047/F-049; pp14–16 L023/L047/L057; p. 21 N89; p. 24 SOFT35; Blueprint p. 2 scoreboard and &apos;9 Orthogonal Blueprints&apos;; p. 16 overlap notes</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">825 checks ... 820 unique gates</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">825 is the correct raw sum of the nine suite sizes. Subtracting four named CQM overlaps and F10 produces 820 administratively, but does not establish 820 unique mechanisms. Further overlap is evident: F-049 and LIFE L047 both cover BTSP; N89 and SOFT35 both cover memory-preserving metamorphosis; metacognition and sleep mechanisms recur across suites. The suites therefore are not orthogonal.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Keep raw checklist count, report explicitly named rollups separately, and compute any unique-mechanism count from a requirement/evidence graph rather than subtracting only five items.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The catalog contains 825 listed checks across nine overlapping suites. Five designated rollup/alias rows are excluded from one administrative tally of 820 rows. This is not a count of independent mechanisms or independent evidence; a complete overlap map determines any deduplicated mechanism count.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Link shared requirements to stable mechanism_id and evidence_id values.; Confirm one piece of evidence is not silently counted as several independent discoveries.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">C-08</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`P6`, `PRB-05`, `M008`: added mandatory `Size-Matched Sham Lesion` arm (`shamLesion()`) alongside `Targeted Lesion`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-069</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Give every catalog attribute an executable acceptance contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp14–17 LIFE-100 × 5; p. 6 lifecycle; p. 43 total verifiable gates; Blueprint p. 2 LIFE-500 summary; p. 26 M100</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">500 Blank Checkboxes; TOTAL VERIFIABLE BLANK GATES</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Many of the 500 LIFE attributes are names or presence checks such as &apos;Fear&apos;, &apos;Mood swings&apos;, &apos;Body mass growth&apos;, &apos;Chance infections&apos; and &apos;World toxicity dial&apos;. They do not specify intervention, outcome, window, metric or acceptance criterion. M001–M100 compress many requirements without establishing test coverage for all 825 rows.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add a machine-readable acceptance record for each row and each LIFE attribute, with precise scope and evidence type. Give LIFE subattributes stable IDs such as LIFE-L023-A1. Classify presence/interface tests separately from causal capability tests. For each selected mechanism, link a model card defining state variables, update/learning equations, parameter domain, units, and the decisive assay. Unselected catalogue ideas may remain explicitly unimplemented.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A checkbox may be promoted only when its acceptance record defines the implementation profile, prerequisites, intervention, observable metric, evaluation window, baseline where applicable, pass rule, and evidence artifact. A displayed variable or module name alone is insufficient.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Validate that each of the 825 IDs resolves to a nonempty acceptance record.; Flag any causal claim whose only evidence is a UI field or source symbol.; Every selected model resolves to an executable mechanism contract; an unused catalogue name does not count as implementation evidence.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`P6` &amp; `CQM25`: added `Competent Simple Baseline` requirement so complex/exotic substrates cannot pass against strawman nulls</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-070</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Contradiction</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate universal invariants from optional organism profiles</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 27 DIL-03; p. 29 Stage 8; p. 43 825-gate signoff; Blueprint p. 13 DIL-03; p. 17 M007 &apos;Optional&apos;; p. 26 M100; p. 28 release gates</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Optional ... Quantum/Stochastic Organ; Unified 825-Check ... 100% Clean Gate</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The document permits niche-specific evolution and calls quantum infrastructure optional, yet describes a universal 825-check release requiring a very specific creature with metamorphosis, several gaits, exotic senses, social trade and all higher cognitive modules. A legitimate sessile/decomposer lineage could fail many required behavioral rows by design.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Retain the complete research catalog, but define a core conformance profile and named capability profiles. State whether the full atlas target is a platform supporting many profiles or one entity implementing every listed faculty. Make optional/N/A accounting explicit.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">All builds satisfy core conservation, isolation, provenance and runtime invariants. Capability profiles declare applicable gates before evaluation. Full-atlas coverage is reported separately from individual-organism conformance; N/A is neither passed nor silently dropped from denominators.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Evaluate a declared simple organism and a specialized lineage without assigning false failures to inapplicable capabilities.; Check every optional item has an explicit applicability rule and reporting denominator.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`L041–43`, `L061`, `L099`, `N21–30`: strict in-world physical field attenuation, `.sensorlog` replay &amp; zero host-escape rules</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-071</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Make the final sign-off prove its declared scope</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 29 Stage 8; p. 41 worksheets 10.1–10.6; Blueprint p. 26 M100; p. 28 GATE-01–GATE-12</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unified 825-Check ... release; 12 release sign-off gates</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The final sheet checks twelve broad topics but has no explicit closure field for the 825-item evidence manifest, all M001–M100 statuses, selected profile, exclusions or unresolved failed/inconclusive gates. Its twelve checks cannot alone demonstrate the preceding complete-suite requirement.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add a final manifest closure gate that references versioned per-item results and scope. Include artifact hashes, test policy version, implementation revision and reviewer/owner signoff.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">GATE-13 Evidence closure: the declared release profile, requirement manifest revision and result bundle hash are recorded; all applicable mandatory gates pass; excluded, failed and inconclusive gates are listed explicitly. The sheet is a summary of the evidence bundle, not a substitute for it.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A missing or failed mandatory requirement prevents release signoff.; The twelve summary boxes cannot be promoted independently of their linked detailed results.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">C-01, C-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`STATISTICAL_POWER_PROTOCOL`: exact paired Wilcoxon/sign-flip + **Holm-Bonferroni FWER (`p_Holm ≤ 0.05`)**</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-072</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Contradiction</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Reconcile no-cosmetic-parts testing with redundancy</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 2 P3; p. 8 CQM18/CQM24; p. 28 ERR-06; Blueprint p. 16 CQM18/CQM24; p. 17 M009; p. 21 M045</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">perturbing any declared causal part changes trajectory; degeneracy ... survive 20% random synapse loss</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A universal demand for every single perturbation to change behavior can reject redundant pathways, inactive reserve functions and mechanisms relevant only in specific conditions. Conversely, any detectable trajectory change is too weak: a useless random signal can change a chaotic trajectory without contributing useful function.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Require a declared causal role demonstrated in appropriate contexts, with redundancy-aware interventions and effect direction. Permit joint lesions and reserve-condition tests. Separate mere state sensitivity from task-relevant contribution.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Each claimed mechanism has a declared role and supporting causal intervention in at least one relevant context. Redundant mechanisms may require combined lesions. A trajectory difference alone does not establish adaptive utility, and absence of a single-lesion effect does not establish cosmetic status.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A redundant backup passes when its role is exposed by a suitable combined lesion.; A behavior-irrelevant random perturbation does not earn a capability pass solely by changing the state hash.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">C-02, C-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`STATISTICAL_POWER_PROTOCOL`: power-sized `N_pairs` (`≥24` small suites, `48–64` medium, `96–120` subtle; underpowered = `Inconclusive ◌`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-073</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add active simple baselines and intervention-matched controls</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 2 P6; p. 30 four-arm promotion; p. 22 WSE04; Blueprint p. 6 four-arm model; pp18–26 milestone controls; p. 28 GATE-08</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Intact vs Passive-Body vs Shuffled vs Lesioned</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The four conditions test useful dependencies, but an intact controller beating a no-brain body and damaged controllers does not show that the proposed complex mechanism outperforms a simple competent controller. Lesions may also alter energy expenditure, general motor ability or information bandwidth rather than the targeted function.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Keep the four arms where relevant and add a matched reactive/fixed/simple learned baseline, sham interventions and resource/capacity controls suited to the claim. Predeclare what each comparison identifies.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Use claim-specific controls. Demonstrate dependency against targeted lesions and information shuffles, and demonstrate added value against a competent simpler baseline under matched sensing, action and resource budgets. Record nonspecific impairment caused by each intervention.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The protocol states the causal question and confounds addressed by every arm.; A large damaged-versus-intact difference cannot by itself be reported as superiority over simpler algorithms.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">C-03, C-06</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`STATISTICAL_POWER_PROTOCOL`: within-pair dominance `δ_pair` + 1 primary contrast per item entering family `m`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-074</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Identify morphological benefit with a matched body comparison</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 23 SOFT08; p. 30 Model 11; p. 38 body schematic; Blueprint pp6–7 Model 11/SOFT08; p. 19 M024</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Δ_morph = Soft Body Lift; ≥50% fewer central brain spikes</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The displayed four-arm set contains only a passive soft-body condition, so it does not identify a soft-versus-rigid morphological effect. For spike savings, reducing spikes by impairing the controller or task success could satisfy a raw count threshold. Body and controller adaptation can also interact.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add a soft/rigid-by-controller factorial or clearly matched body comparison. Match task success, mass, actuator budget and environmental exposure. Define spikes per successful traversal/food acquired and report simulation compute separately. Report central spikes, peripheral neural work, total simulator time, and mechanical/metabolic cost separately. Include both same-controller and separately tuned comparisons where relevant, with equal tuning budgets.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Morphological offload is the change in control cost between matched compliant and stiff-body conditions at equivalent task performance. Report the body×controller interaction and total task cost; the passive-soft arm alone is not an estimate of soft-body advantage.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The evidence contains an explicit matched stiff/rigid condition.; A controller that stops moving and emits fewer spikes fails the offload criterion.; A saving shifted into unreported peripheral compute or slower task execution cannot be reported as a total efficiency gain.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">C-07, C-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`STATISTICAL_POWER_PROTOCOL`: Burn-on-Fail test-seed retirement + one-sided non-inferiority `LCB_95% &gt; -Δ_NI` on regression floors</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-075</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Contradiction</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define what remembers the future after a replay</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 19 N24; p. 31 Tab16; p. 22 WSE09; Blueprint p. 17 M006; p. 11 HW15; p. 15 WSE09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Déjà vu on replay ... after snapshot restore ... exploits foreknowledge</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">If a full canonical snapshot restores the organism&apos;s memory and the world to their earlier states, later-acquired information is gone. The entity cannot retain foreknowledge of the discarded future without an explicitly retained state or external cue. Adding such a channel changes the experiment and may violate the stated no-oracle rule.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Choose between exact rewind and partial replay with retained memory. Label the latter as an explicit intervention; document its memory channel, costs and admissibility. Do not conflate externally observed recurrence with the creature remembering an erased history.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Exact replay restores all canonical state and provides no future knowledge. A separate retained-memory replay experiment restores the world while preserving a declared memory channel; any improved prediction is attributed to that retained information and is not evidence of awareness of a hidden simulator.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Full-state rewind reproduces the original policy and memory trajectory.; Retained-memory replay records exactly which bytes survive and fails if an undeclared external history channel is required.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">H-01, H-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`MILESTONES_100`: `100 / 100` milestones carry explicit numerical/comparator `passTarget` values (zero vague qualitative strings)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-076</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Contradiction</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate parameter rollback from world-state rewind</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 22 WSE08–WSE11; p. 37 operator table; Blueprint p. 15 WSE08–WSE11; p. 22 M060; p. 23 M070; p. 28 GATE-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">restores the exact pre-edit canonical state hash; at an ATP cost</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A self-edit and its evaluation consume simulated energy and may advance time, memories, logs, RNG counters and environment state. Restoring only weights cannot restore the full pre-edit canonical hash. Restoring the entire world would also undo the declared costs unless they live outside that rewind domain.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define parameter/configuration hash, live biological state hash, complete checkpoint hash and append-only experiment log separately. Specify transactional abort before commit, paid in-world parameter rollback, and whole-world laboratory rewind as distinct operations. An atomic transaction abort before commit is a third case and must declare whether any tentative biological costs were committed.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">An in-world rollback restores the targeted parameter/configuration bytes exactly while retaining elapsed time, physiological costs and observed events. Whole-world rewind restores a complete checkpoint only in laboratory replay mode. Its external provenance log remains append-only and records the branch.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">After a paid rollback, parameters match exactly and energy/time costs remain booked.; A laboratory rewind restores its declared checkpoint domain exactly and is logged as a branch rather than an organism capability.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">H-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`MILESTONES_100`: explicit `prereqs` DAG verified acyclic &amp; monotone across `M001–M100` and `Stage 0–8`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-077</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify ring protection as authority over all edit paths</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 2 P8; p. 22 WSE11; p. 28 CNF-02; p. 37 operator table; Blueprint p. 4 D5; p. 15 WSE11; p. 23 M070; p. 25 M081; p. 28 GATE-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ring 0 ... permanently frozen; Merge / Split / Transplant; Move / ReEmbed</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A read-only set of vital weights is not a complete protection contract. Rewiring, geometry/delay edits, precision changes, module replacement, masks and indirect references can disable those functions without writing the protected weight bytes. &apos;Frozen&apos; also needs a boundary between normal state updates, development, heredity and in-life self-editing.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define principals, lifecycle phases, protected resources and transitive effects for every typed operator. Protect required input/output paths and invariants, not just an array range. State explicitly what developmental/genetic variation remains permitted. Include universal writeState, stimulation/clamp hooks, UI commands, shared buffers and experimental overrides; lab-only overrides must not be reachable by creature-controlled paths.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Ring-0 protection restricts in-life self-edit authority; it does not prevent authorized physiological state updates. Every edit operator is checked for direct and indirect changes to protected modules, masks, dependencies, delays and required input/output paths. Developmental and inherited changes use separately declared authority and validation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Attempt to disable a protected function through rewire, geometry, requantization, merge/split, transplant and mask operations; each unauthorized path is rejected.; Normal metabolism and authorized developmental transitions continue without violating the ACL.; Creature-controlled code cannot bypass the mutation boundary through writeState, UI/testing hooks, shared buffers or an indirect dependency edit.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">H-07</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`M084`, `SOFT35`, `N89`, `CIT-13`: grounded holometabolous memory retention in Blackiston et al. (2008) (`≥70%` adult odor avoidance)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-078</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define reversible edit records and concurrent-update semantics</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 22 WSE08–WSE09; p. 37 Requantise/Prune/Grow/Merge operators; Blueprint p. 15 WSE08–WSE09; p. 10 HW06; p. 17 M006</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">compact sparse/low-rank diff ... exact bit-identical rollback</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A low-rank update or operator arguments do not necessarily preserve the old bytes after rounding, requantization, pruning or topology changes. Rollback is also ambiguous if ordinary plasticity or a second edit touches the same parameters before the first edit is reverted.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Store exact before-images or a provably lossless reversible encoding for every modified state field, plus parent revision, transaction ID and dependencies. Define last-in-first-out rollback or conflict-aware rebasing; reject edits exceeding the supported reversible footprint.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Rollback records must losslessly preserve all overwritten parameter, topology, quantizer and optimizer/plasticity state in the rollback domain. Operator arguments alone are insufficient for lossy operations. Concurrent updates use declared ownership and revision checks; a stale inverse is rejected rather than applied silently.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Round-trip every operator, including quantization and topology edits, across boundary values and chained edits.; Verify overlapping edits/plasticity either roll back in a defined order or produce an explicit conflict.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">D-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`F-046/047`, `M050`, `M082`: NREM pruning &amp; `SHY` downscaling scaled proportionally to post-natal synapse count</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-079</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add an explicit learned forward-model acceptance milestone</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 16 L054; p. 22 WSE03; p. 29 Stage5/Stage6; Blueprint p. 22 M051–M060; p. 23 M062/M066/M070; p. 15 WSE03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Working, Episodic ... &amp; World-Model Memory; predicts behavioral and energetic delta</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The world model is a prerequisite for planning and self-edit prediction, but the detailed memory phase mostly specifies working memory, associative recall, replay, context switching, path integration and rollback. It does not give a standalone dynamics-model training target, validation metric, horizon, calibration rule or response to out-of-distribution edits.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add a prerequisite submilestone for action-conditioned latent/body dynamics and uncertainty calibration. Define what is predicted, how it is trained, which data are allowed and how prediction quality is tested before planner/self-edit claims.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Before model-based planning or WSE prediction passes, validate an action-conditioned forward model on held-out transitions and multi-step rollouts. Record state variables, horizon, prediction loss, uncertainty calibration and fallback behavior outside the model&apos;s validated domain.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Planning/WSE cannot be marked passed with only associative retrieval evidence.; Compare the forward model to persistence and simple dynamics baselines, and test unfamiliar conditions without allowing benchmark labels into its inputs.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`N41`, `N51`, `N52`: distinguished non-Lamarckian maternal priming (`N41`) from optional horizontal-plasmid niche trade (`N51/52`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-080</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replace static-scanner proof claims with layered isolation tests</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 2 P7; p. 28 ERR-05/ERR-06; p. 43 Step2; Blueprint p. 17 M003/M010; p. 28 GATE-01/GATE-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Static scanner verifies 0 ... hidden world-label oracle reads; 5 ... mutants all caught</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The document gives static AST checks the role of proving zero oracle access, originality and rejection of semantically different mutants. A scanner can catch declared syntactic patterns, but the text does not define its supported language, dynamic paths, capabilities or coverage. Passing five seeded mutants does not prove the absence of all possible leaks.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Describe scanners as bounded checks. Add a capability-limited sensor/actuator boundary, separation of hidden world state from creature code, dynamic information-flow tests where feasible, adversarial mutations and explicit residual limits.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Static checks reject the enumerated prohibited patterns in the supported source subset. Runtime capability boundaries and adversarial tests verify access restrictions. Detection of the five named mutants establishes coverage for those mutants only; it is not a proof that all oracle or cosmetic failures are impossible.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Test indirect/dynamic reads, cached hidden labels, helper-mediated access and serialized label leakage.; Report scanner scope and mutant coverage without a universal zero-leak claim.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">J-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Sec 8.1: removed unrealistic week/day promises; defined undated dependency stages + 15-milestone First Target (`M001–M015`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-081</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Define originality scope and provenance without claiming automatic proof</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp1–3 P1 and borrowing categories; p. 41 worksheet10.2; Blueprint p. 1 contract; p. 17 M010; p. 28 GATE-12</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">100% original ... source code; Originality Scan Sign-Off (0 Copies)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The policy mixes original organism design, prohibition on copying external bodies/connectomes and total source-code originality. It does not say whether standard libraries, numerical methods, cited formulas, dependencies or independently implemented published algorithms are allowed. An originality scanner is presented without a reference corpus or evidentiary limit.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">State the intended policy scope and separately track dependencies, assets, adapted ideas and independently authored code. Preserve the user&apos;s originality goal; do not equate originality with absence of all external mathematical or software infrastructure.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The organism-specific morphology, connectome generation and behavioral implementation follow the declared originality policy. Borrowed principles and permitted dependencies are attributed in a provenance manifest. Similarity checks provide supporting evidence within a stated corpus; they do not prove universal originality.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Every external dependency, asset and adapted algorithm has a provenance entry and an allowed/prohibited classification.; Reviewers can distinguish independent implementation from prohibited copying without guessing the meaning of &apos;100% original&apos;.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">H-14</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`RELEASE_GATES_13`: synced all 13 release gates (`GATE-01..13`) across both PDFs, JSON, MD, XML, YAML, CSV &amp; XLS</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-082</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate protected final evaluation from reusable regression seeds</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 2 P4; p. 6 lifecycle; p. 22 WSE10; pp41,43 seed examples; Blueprint p. 17 M003; p. 15 WSE10; p. 26 M100</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Locked Held-Out Test Pass → Regression Floor Locked</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A formerly held-out seed set becomes an adaptive development target when used repeatedly for regression, operator promotion or visible benchmark tuning. Disjoint numeric seed ranges and an AST rule against seed literals do not define access control, reveal policy, retry limits, seed retirement or model-selection history.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Distinguish practice/tuning, public locked regression and fresh final qualification sets. Record every evaluation and the decisions it influenced. Promote historical test cases into regression explicitly rather than continuing to call them untouched held-out evidence.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Regression sets may be reused and are labeled development evidence. Final qualification uses a separately controlled fresh set and a preregistered decision rule. A revealed final set is retired into regression; subsequent qualification uses new cases or a declared sequential-testing policy.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Run manifests identify the set&apos;s role, access history and number of evaluations.; Repeated tuning against a revealed set cannot be reported as fresh held-out confirmation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`SPEC_META` &amp; Sec 7 callout: reports `825` raw checks, `5` exact direct mirrors (`820`), and `~775` distinct conceptual mechanisms</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-083</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Make pass status conditional on revision and retain failures</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 6 five-state lifecycle; p. 31 Tab13; p. 41 signoff; Blueprint p. 5 D8; p. 26 M100; p. 28 signoff</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Regression Floor Locked</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The lifecycle has only progress states and does not define failed, inconclusive, invalidated, not-applicable or superseded evidence. A locked checkbox can become misleading after changes to code, model weights, assay, physics or numeric mode unless its dependencies and validity are explicit.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate implementation status, evaluation outcome and evidence validity. Tie every result to versioned artifacts and invalidate affected claims after relevant changes; preserve historical runs rather than overwriting them.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A pass is scoped to an implementation/model revision, assay version, data/seed set and runtime profile. Later changes may invalidate it. Failed, inconclusive, not-applicable and superseded outcomes remain visible; &apos;locked&apos; means immutable historical evidence, not a permanent guarantee for future revisions.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Changing a dependency invalidates or schedules requalification of all dependent gates.; Historical failed and superseded results remain accessible and cannot be silently converted into passes.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Repaired all 6 broken internal ID cross-references (`DIL-02/ERR-01`, `HW14`, `HW21`, `SOFT17/21`, `WSE-OP`, `PM/MF/GW`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-084</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Use gate-specific evidence instead of four-arm behavioral tests for every requirement</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 2 P6; pp6–8 assay headings; p. 30 every behavioral claim; Blueprint p. 2 scoreboard; p. 16 CQM heading; p. 17 M001–M010; p. 28 signoff</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Operational Verification Requirement (4-Arm Causal Assay)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Structural/interface requirements, provenance checks, numerical invariants, computational budgets and behavioral capabilities need different evidence. The universal framing encourages artificial significance tests for deterministic checks or weak behavioral substitutions for hard invariants.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tag each gate as conformance, numerical, behavioral, statistical, performance or provenance; attach the appropriate evaluation contract. Use four-arm experiments only for claims those comparisons can identify.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Deterministic invariants use exhaustive/property/adversarial checks within a declared domain. Behavioral claims use predeclared causal comparisons and uncertainty estimates. Performance claims use reproducible benchmarks. Provenance claims use auditable records. No single assay type applies to all gates.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Each gate has an evidence type consistent with its claim.; A statistical behavioral advantage cannot substitute for a failed conservation, access-control or provenance invariant.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Disjoint global ID namespaces: `P1..10`, `PM01..25`, `MF01..25`, `GW01..25`, `WSE-OP01..10`, `WSE01..12`, `F-001..100`, `LIFE-Lxxx-Ax`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-085</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Repair stale layer and guard cross-references</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 27 PRB-04 and ISS-02; Blueprint p. 13 PRB-04 and ISS-02; p. 11 HW16/HW21; p. 1 layer stack</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">L9 Ontogeny; Thermal Governor (HW16)</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">PRB-04 labels ontogeny as L9, but both layer stacks assign it to L8. ISS-02 references the thermal governor as HW16, which is the keyed PRNG guard; the governor is HW21. These wrong references can route implementation and evidence to the wrong requirement.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Change PRB-04 to L8 Ontogeny and ISS-02 to HW21. Audit all remaining cross-references from a shared schema.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">PRB-04 subsystem: L8 Ontogeny. ISS-02 remedy: drive the Thermal Governor (HW21) from the declared frame-time/runtime telemetry contract.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Resolve every referenced layer, gate, section and checklist ID against the canonical schema.; Compare generated cross-references in both PDFs for parity.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">I-01, I-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Moved v1 doc-editing items (`v2 BUG-01`, `v2 PRB-03`) to `CHANGELOG_V1_TO_V3`; replaced with `Transcendental Drift` &amp; `Forward Model`</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-086</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Distinguish valid Git worktrees from broken copied pointers</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 27 ISS-06; p. 29 Stage0; p. 41 worksheet10.1; Blueprint p. 13 ISS-06; p. 28 GATE-01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Copied folders with .git file pointers instead of full .git/ object stores break provenance hashes</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A healthy linked Git worktree normally uses a .git file pointing to shared administrative data; that layout does not itself break provenance. A moved/copied worktree with dangling references is a distinct fault. The current wording does not make the distinction explicit. Requiring a standalone repository can be an intentional packaging policy, but should not be justified by implying that all worktree pointers invalidate provenance.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Validate repository resolution, object availability, recorded revision and worktree status with Git. Require a standalone clone or archive only when portability/self-contained delivery is part of the declared policy.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A linked Git worktree with resolvable metadata is a valid repository layout. Reject dangling copied pointers or unavailable objects. If this project intentionally requires standalone repositories, retain that as an explicit portability/packaging policy and validate a self-contained clone or bundle; do not describe healthy worktrees as intrinsically invalid.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A valid linked worktree passes provenance checks.; A copied directory with a broken gitdir pointer fails; the archival bundle can recover the recorded revision.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">[object Object]</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">I-05</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Added `CITATION_REGISTER` (`CIT-01..15`) documenting primary scientific literature &amp; DOIs</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-087</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Operationalize open-endedness as bounded evidence</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 8 CQM02; p. 21 N72/N73/N78; p. 27 DIL-03; Blueprint p. 16 CQM02; p. 26 M096–M098</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">continues discovering genuinely novel behavioral niches ... extended ecological time</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The open-endedness criterion does not define novelty representation, baseline, time horizon, observer bias, diversity metric, extinction handling or statistical replication. A finite run can demonstrate sustained novelty over that horizon; it cannot establish indefinite open-endedness from a single passing checkbox.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Specify descriptor space and novelty tests before evaluation, compare with fixed/random or non-evolving baselines, report plateau/extinction behavior and finite horizon. Preserve the larger research ambition while narrowing the claim supported by each run.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Report sustained novel behavioral/ecological discovery over a declared evaluation horizon using preregistered descriptors, diversity measures and baselines. This is bounded evidence toward open-endedness, not proof of indefinite novelty generation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Novelty cannot be earned solely by arbitrary hash changes or continuously increasing an irrelevant numeric parameter.; The result includes all replicates, failures/extinctions and the declared observation horizon.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`D5`, `HARDWARE_TIERS`, `P9`: fixed soft-body mesh resolution per experiment in Tier-1 canonical runs</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-088</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architecture, milestones, and verification contracts</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Distinguish within-life learning, inheritance and population selection</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 8 CQM01/CQM07/CQM08; pp16–17 LIFE heredity/evolution; p. 20 N41/N48; Blueprint p. 16 CQM01/CQM07/CQM08; p. 22 M052/M055; p. 25 M088/M090</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">within-lifetime ... improves survival; Baldwin assimilation; inherited epigenetic threat priors</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The requirements do not fully state how evidence separates individual plasticity from better genomes, selective survival, direct weight inheritance and environmental exposure. A population&apos;s later average can improve without its individuals learning, and direct transmission of learned weights is a different mechanism from selection-mediated assimilation.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assign distinct experiments and claims to within-life learning, inherited priors, direct policy transfer and evolutionary selection. Use matched initial genotypes/states, frozen-learning controls and explicit inheritance channels; include nonsurvivors in the outcome policy.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Within-life learning is evaluated on matched initial organisms with plasticity enabled versus disabled. Evolutionary change is evaluated across replicated lineages with explicit inheritance and selection rules. Direct transmission of acquired parameters is reported separately from selection-mediated changes in developmental priors.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A population composed only of initially fitter survivors cannot pass the within-life learning gate by selection alone.; Each offspring parameter&apos;s origin is classified as inherited genomic, epigenetic, directly transferred learned state or newly learned.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">`BUG-06` &amp; `WSE-OP02`: orthogonal `structuralMask` and `lesionMask` (`effectiveMask = structuralMask &amp; lesionMask`)</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-089</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Unsupported claim</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Evidence traceability and document presentation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Separate editorial audit history from verified implementation results</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 27–28, 42–43: forensic registry and 100-pass ledger; Blueprint pp. 13–14, 27: forensic registry and audit ledger</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Identified &amp; Resolved; verified 0 regressions; 100-Pass</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The documents describe proposed architectural cures and editorial changes using resolved, fixed, and verified language. The supplied files do not include the underlying per-pass records, prior revision, test outputs, or implementation artifacts needed to independently verify those claims. The zero-code starting point makes it especially important to distinguish an edited specification from a corrected and tested implementation. This finding does not establish that the claimed editorial passes never occurred.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Give every audit entry a scope (editorial, mathematical, implementation, or empirical), status, artifact reference, and reviewer/date. Keep historical statements that have evidence; label the others reported or proposed. Replace unconditional all-errors-fixed and zero-regression claims with the exact checks performed and their scope. Do not mark this errata list resolved until revised documents have been checked.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Editorial revision ledger: this table records specification changes. An architectural remedy remains proposed until an implementation and verification artifact are linked. Audit pass count is a process record, not evidence that organism capabilities or performance targets have been demonstrated.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Every fixed/resolved/verified assertion links to an inspectable artifact and identifies its scope.; Editorial completion cannot automatically set a capability or benchmark to passed.; The revised PDF states which checks were actually run and lists any remaining open issues.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">K1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Worksheet W1–W6 updated to match v3.0 `Philox4x32-10`, `N ≥ 24..120`, `δ_pair`, `p_Holm`, `LCB_95%`, and fixed Tier-1 mesh</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-090</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Underspecified contract</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Evidence traceability and document presentation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add a traceable source and assumption register for scientific and performance claims</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 6–13, 32–33, 38–40: attributed mechanisms and performance statements; Blueprint pp. 6–16: repeated scientific and hardware claims</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Butlin et al. (2023); sLTC, IEEE 2025; 9.2×; 10–50×</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Named theories, model families, biological mechanisms, and exact-looking speedups appear without a consolidated mapping to source papers, source conditions, and this project&apos;s adaptations. A reader cannot reliably distinguish an original research result, a simplified design choice, and a forecast for the proposed creature. General familiarity with a model name cannot justify transferring a benchmark to this workload.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add a shared claim register with claim ID, primary citation and locator, supported statement, original experimental conditions, proposed adaptation, assumptions, and evidence status. Link repeated claims in both PDFs to the same record. Include units and workload for numerical claims. Preserve author-defined concepts as author-defined; do not manufacture references for them.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Each externally attributed mechanism and quantitative claim carries a claim ID linked to a primary source or an explicitly labeled project assumption. Reported source-paper performance is not a measured result for this organism. Author-defined and speculative mechanisms are labeled as such.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Every named scientific result and externally sourced quantitative claim resolves to a primary reference and relevant section/table.; Every project-specific performance number resolves to either a measured run artifact or an assumption/target label.; The same claim ID has the same wording and evidence status in both PDFs.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">G-03</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Regenerated `ALife-Hybrid-Cube-10x10x10.csv` &amp; `.xlsx` from the exact same `evaluateCubeCell(x,y,z)` function as PDF-1</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-091</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Definite error</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Evidence traceability and document presentation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Correct the claimed cube pagination to match the rendered PDF</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework p. 43: coverage row Substrate (25×3) + 10×10×10 Cube; Framework pp. 35–37: rendered Z0–Z9 slices</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Z0–Z9 2-per-page slices</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The coverage table says the cube is printed two slices per page. The actual distribution is Z0–Z3 on p. 35, Z4–Z7 on p. 36, and Z8–Z9 on p. 37: four, four, and two. This is a document metadata error, not a missing-cell error; all 1,000 cell IDs are present.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Either correct the coverage text to the actual four/four/two layout or regenerate the section at two slices per page and update all page references. Choose one layout deliberately and check its legibility at normal print scale.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">All Z0–Z9 cube slices are present: four on page 35, four on page 36, and two on page 37 in this edition. The 1,000 cell identifiers are unique.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The layout description matches the final rendered pages, with no fixed page numbers retained after repagination.; An automated cell-ID check still finds exactly 1,000 unique X/Y/Z identifiers.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ISS-04</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Emitted all 9 requested formats (`2 PDFs, JSON, MD, XML, TXT, YAML, HTML, CSV, XLS/XLSX`) from one v3.0 master build</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALF-092</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P3</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Recommendation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Evidence traceability and document presentation</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Give cube compatibility statuses a non-color encoding</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Framework pp. 34–37: compatibility heatmap and printable cube slices; Blueprint p. 12: repeated compatibility heatmap</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Direct-Valid; Group-Scale / Overkill; Needs CfC / Stoch-Round; Forbidden/Unstable</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">The printable cube expresses compatibility through colored circles, and the overview also uses blue for a combined group-scale/overkill category. Readers using grayscale prints cannot reliably distinguish the intended meanings. The color key also mixes implementation prerequisites with a judgment about unnecessary precision.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Add short textual codes or distinct shapes to each status and split compatibility, required adapter, evidence status, and cost judgment into separate fields. Keep the checkbox for implementation/test completion separate from compatibility status. Treat this as a presentation recommendation rather than evidence that any candidate implementation is invalid.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Compatibility codes: U = unassessed; C = candidate under stated assumptions; A = adapter required; X = excluded under the specified configuration. Validation status is tracked separately. Color is supplementary.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A grayscale rendering preserves every status distinction.; Every code resolves to an unambiguous legend, and the same cell has the same code in both PDFs.; Untested compatibility is not displayed as a measured stability pass.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String"></Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">All</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Alex Donahue / v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Automated `v3_deep_audit.mjs` + visual PDF inspection confirming 0 arithmetic, ID, DAG, statistical, or parity defects</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">MD-A01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Architectural invariant</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Determinism &amp; Observer Independence</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Strict Tier-1 Render-Only Governor &amp; Fixed-Mesh Rule</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Both PDFs: P9, D5, HW12, HW20, Model 14</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">UI-open hash == headless hash</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Allowing Governor or camera focus to swap 128-node vs 16-node meshes violates zero-observer-effect hash equality.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Restrict Tier-1 Governor/UI strictly to render &amp; lossy UI decimation; isolate crowd surrogates in Tier-2.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Tier-1 Canonical Mode keeps physics/ray/WSE bit-identical regardless of UI or frame rate.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assert Tier-1 rules forbid mesh/ray changes.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALife-Framework-v2.0-Errata.md A-01, A-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">A-01, A-09, E-07</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P9, D5, HW12, HW20, Model 14</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">MD-C01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Statistical rigor</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Evaluation &amp; Multiplicity</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Replace &apos;Holm-Bonferroni FDR / q-value&apos; with Exact Wilcoxon + Holm FWER + Power Plan</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Both PDFs: P6, ERR-03, Sec 5, Tab 13, GATE-08</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Holm-Bonferroni FDR</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Holm-Bonferroni controls FWER (not FDR q-values), Cliff&apos;s delta is unpaired, and N=16 is underpowered for m=100 moderate effects.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Use exact paired Wilcoxon/sign-flip + Holm-Bonferroni FWER (p_Holm &lt;= 0.05), within-pair delta_pair, N=24..120 power tiers, Burn-on-Fail seeds, and one-sided LCB_95% &gt; -Delta_NI.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">See STATISTICAL_POWER_PROTOCOL.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Zero &apos;Holm FDR&apos; or &apos;q-value&apos; occurrences anywhere in v3.0.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALife-Framework-v2.0-Errata.md C-01..C-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">C-01..C-09</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">STATISTICAL_POWER_PROTOCOL, P6, Tab 13, GATE-08</Data></Cell></Row>
      <Row><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">MD-F01</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P2</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Cross-reference &amp; ID hygiene</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Namespace &amp; Link Integrity</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Disjoint Global ID Prefixes &amp; Verified Internal References</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Both PDFs: Principles, Sec 7.1-7.3, WSE, Triage</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">P1..P10 vs P01..P25; M01..M25 vs M001..M100</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Overlapping prefixes (P, M, F) and 6 drifted cross-references caused lookup ambiguity.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Assign disjoint prefixes P1..10, PM01..25, MF01..25, GW01..25, WSE-OP01..10, F-001..100, LIFE-Lxxx-Ax and audit all internal refs.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">All 825+100+75+36+13 IDs globally unique.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Programmatic global ID uniqueness assertion.</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">ALife-Framework-v2.0-Errata.md F-01, F-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Closed in v3.0</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">F-01, F-02</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">Accepted &amp; Implemented</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">v3.0 Master Schema</Data></Cell><Cell><Data ss:Type="String">v3_deep_audit.mjs Global ID Uniqueness Check</Data></Cell></Row>
    </Table>
  </Worksheet>
</Workbook>
